hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"606","Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah reported to us, from Ata, concerning worms coming out of a person: wudu is performed for it. From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ata, who said: He informed us, and we adopt it."
"607","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, who said: My father informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was invited to food and ate a shoulder, then the muezzin came to him, so he stood for prayer and did not perform wudu."
"608","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ja'far ibn Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, from his father, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut meat from a shoulder and eat, then the muezzin came to him, so he threw down the knife, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu."
"609","Ma'mar reported to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu, then cut a shoulder and ate, then went to prayer and did not perform wudu."
"610","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from a man of the Ansar, from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat from a sheep's shoulder, then stand for prayer and not perform wudu."
"611","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was eating a bone with meat on it, the muezzin came to him, so he put it down, stood for prayer, and did not touch water."
"612","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed me that Ata ibn Yasar informed him that Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that she brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a roasted side, and he ate from it, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu."
"613","Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj reported to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir reported to us, who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Bread and meat were brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then he called for wudu water and performed wudu, then prayed Zuhr; then he called for the remainder of his food and ate, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu. He said: Then I entered with Abu Bakr, and he said: Is there anything? By Allah, he did not find it, so he said: Where is your sheep? It was brought, so he held it, then milked for us and made hays for us, and we ate; then we stood for prayer and did not perform wudu. Then I entered with Umar, and a bowl with bread and meat was placed here, and a bowl with bread and meat here, and Umar ate, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu. Abd al-Razzaq reported to us, who said: Ma'mar reported to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, the like of it."
"614","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, that Ali would not perform wudu because of what fire had touched."
"615","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed me that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah; and he saw Abu Hurayrah performing wudu, then he said: O Ibn Abbas, do you know from what I perform wudu? He said: No. He said: I performed wudu because of pieces of dried yogurt I ate. Ibn Abbas said: I do not care from what you perform wudu; I bear witness that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat a shoulder of meat, then stand for prayer and not perform wudu. He said: And Sulayman was present at that from both of them."
"616","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, that Uthman ibn Affan ate food touched by fire, then went to prayer and did not perform wudu. He said: And I know only that he said: Then Uthman said: I performed wudu as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu, and I ate as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate, and I prayed as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed."
"617","From al-Thawri, from Abu Awn, who said: Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had narrated to us, who said: Abu Hurayrah said: Wudu is for what fire has touched. Marwan said: How can anyone be asked while among us are the wives of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), our mothers? He said: So he sent me to Umm Salamah and I asked her, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, having performed wudu, and I handed him a bone or a shoulder, and he ate, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu."
"618","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to prayer and saw one of his children with a bone with meat, so he took it and bit from it, then went on and prayed."
"619","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from his maternal uncle, who said: Ibn Abbas would have a mat spread for him on Friday in the house of his maternal aunt Maymunah, and he would narrate. A man said to him: Inform me about what fire has touched. Ibn Abbas said: I will inform you only of what I saw of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he and his Companions were in his house, and the muezzin came to him, so he stood for prayer; when he was at the door, he was met with a tray with bread and meat in it, so he returned with his Companions and ate, and they ate; then he returned to prayer and did not perform wudu."
"620","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, ate a shoulder of meat or a foreleg, then stood and prayed for us and did not perform wudu. Ata said: And I thought that Jabir said: And he did not rinse his mouth nor wash his hand. He said: I thought he said: He wiped his hand."
"621","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Abu Bakr ate bread and meat, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu."
"622","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: We ate bread and meat with Abu Bakr, then he stood for prayer and did not perform wudu. Ma'mar said: I think he said: Except that he rinsed his mouth."
"623","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: A bowl was brought to us while we were with Ibn Mas'ud, and he ordered it placed in the road, and he ate from it and we ate with him, and he kept inviting whoever passed by him; then we went to prayer, and he did no more than wash his fingertips and rinse his mouth, then prayed."
"624","From Ma'mar, from Ibn al-Munkadir, who said: I heard him narrating from Jabir that Umar ate from a bowl, then stood and prayed and did not perform wudu."
"625","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: A dish was brought to us from the house of Ibn Mas'ud with bread and meat in it, and we ate, and Ibn Mas'ud was with us; and he rinsed his mouth and washed his fingers at Maghrib."
"626","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: Fire is only the blessing of Allah; it does not make anything lawful nor unlawful, and there is no wudu for what fire has touched, nor wudu for what enters; wudu is only for what comes out of a person. As for his saying it does not make anything lawful, it is for their saying: when fire touches date-syrup it becomes lawful; and his saying it does not make it unlawful is for their saying: wudu is for what fire has touched."
"627","Ata said: And I heard Ibn Abbas saying to a person asking him about that: If you perform wudu for what fire has touched, then hot water is bathed with. He would see no harm in bathing with hot water, and would perform wudu with it; and oils have been touched by fire, so do not perform wudu because of them."
"628","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me that he said: We would sometimes visit Ibn Abbas, and he would bring his dinner near sunset, and he would dine and we would dine, and he would do no more than wash his hands and rinse his mouth, and not perform wudu, then pray."
"629","From al-Thawri, from Yazid, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he was asked about wudu for what fire has touched, and he said: Fire has only added goodness to it."
"630","From Ma'mar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, freedman of Ibn Abbas, who said: We were with Ibn Abbas in his house, and he brought food near to us, and the call to prayer was made. He said: When this is present, begin with it. The people ate, and one of them said: Shall we not perform wudu? Ibn Abbas said to him: It is said: Wudu is for what fire has touched. He said: Fire has only added goodness to it; had fire not touched it, you would not eat it. He said: Then he prayed with us on a carpet or a mat that covered his house."
"631","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Were it not for smacking the lips, I would not care not to rinse my mouth."
"632","From al-Thawri, from Wa'il ibn Dawud, from Ibrahim, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: Wudu is only for what comes out, and fasting is for what enters, and not for what comes out."
"633","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: Uthman ibn Umar al-Taymi informed me, from Uqbah ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: I arrived in Madinah and dined with Abu Talhah before Maghrib, and with him were a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Ubayy ibn Ka'b. Maghrib came, so I stood to perform wudu, and they said: What is this Iraqi practice you have introduced? For wholesome foods you perform wudu? So they all prayed Maghrib and did not perform wudu."
"634","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I asked Abidah about what fire has touched, so he ordered a sheep and it was slaughtered, then a need hurried him; I think the governor called him. He called for milk, ghee and bread, and ate, and we ate with him; then he stood for prayer and prayed, and did not perform wudu. Ibn Sirin said: So I thought he only intended to show me that."
"635","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, that he would eat tharid, then pray and not perform wudu."
"636","From Ma'mar ibn Sulayman, from Abu Ghalib, who said: I would eat tharid and meat with Abu Umamah, and he would pray and not perform wudu."
"637","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: Wudu is only for what comes out. He said: And not for what enters, because it enters while pure, you have nothing from it; and it comes out while impure, upon you is wudu and purification for it."
"638","Yahya ibn Rabi'ah reported to us, who said: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah saying: Jabir ibn Abdullah informed me that Abu Bakr ate a sheep's shoulder or foreleg, then stood for prayer and did not perform wudu. It was said to him: Shall we bring you wudu water? He said: I have not innovated a hadath."
"639","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sufyan ibn al-Mughirah ibn al-Akhnas, that he entered upon Umm Habibah and she gave him sawiq to drink; then he stood to pray, and she said to him: Perform wudu, O son of my brother, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Perform wudu for what fire has touched."
"640","Ma'mar said: al-Zuhri said: It reached me that Zayd ibn Thabit and A'ishah would perform wudu for what fire has touched."
"641","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sufyan ibn Sa'id ibn al-Akhnas, from Umm Habibah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform wudu for what fire has touched. Al-Zuhri said: And it reached me, from Zayd ibn Thabit and from A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"642","Ma'mar reported to us, from al-Zuhri, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz, who said: I passed by Abu Hurayrah while he was performing wudu, and he said: Do you know from what I perform wudu? From pieces of dried yogurt I ate; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Perform wudu for what fire has touched."
"643","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me that Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz informed him that he found Abu Hurayrah performing wudu at the back of the mosque. Abu Hurayrah said: I only perform wudu because of pieces of dried yogurt I ate, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform wudu for what fire has touched."
"644","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: I do not care whether I dip my hands in dung and blood, or eat food touched by fire, then pray without performing wudu. He said: And al-Hasan used to adopt it."
"645","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, who said: I saw Anas ibn Malik come out from al-Hajjaj while talking to himself. I said: What is the matter with you, O Abu Hamzah? He said: I came out from this man, and he called for food for the people, and he ate and they ate; then they stood for prayer and did not perform wudu, or he said: they did not touch water. Anas would perform wudu for what fire had altered."
"646","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he would perform wudu for what fire had touched, even performing wudu for sugar."
"647","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, who said: Abu Hurayrah would perform wudu for what fire had touched, and that reached Ibn Abbas, so he sent to him, saying: What if I take a pleasant oil and oil my beard with it; would I be one performing wudu? Abu Hurayrah said: O son of my brother, when you narrate a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not strike examples for it in argument. Abu Bakr said: Ma'mar and al-Zuhri would perform wudu for what fire had touched."
"648","From al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he would perform wudu for what fire had touched."
"649","Urwah, from A'ishah, that she would perform wudu for what fire had touched."
"650","Ma'mar reported to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab would bathe with hot water."
"651","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would perform wudu with hot water."
"652","Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: There is no harm in bathing with hot water and performing wudu from it."
"653","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Is it disliked to bathe with hot water and perform wudu with it? He said: No."
"654","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Were you one performing wudu because of meat and washing your hand of its trace? He said: Yes. I said: With ushnaan or with water? He said: Rather with water; ushnaan is only something they introduced. I said: What about grease, ghee, syrup or fat you ate from; were you washing your hand of it or rinsing your mouth? He said: No. I said: From bread alone? He said: I neither rinse my mouth from it nor wash my hands."
"655","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Green fruits and bananas? He said: I would not wash my hands from them nor rinse my mouth, unless something of them sticking to my hands disgusts me; but for other than that, no. I said: Why do you rinse your mouth for meat apart from that? He said: Meat gets into the molars and teeth. I said: What if you knew nothing of it entered your teeth; would you care not to rinse? He said: No, by Allah, I would not care not to rinse from it ever."
"656","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Abu Bakr brought us bread and meat, and we ate; then he called for water and rinsed his mouth and did not perform wudu, then stood for prayer."
"657","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Muhayriz, who said: Perform wudu for what fire has touched, rinse from milk, and do not rinse from fruit."
"658","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank milk and rinsed his mouth, and said: It has fat in it."
"659","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Abu Ghalib, that Abu Umamah would rinse his mouth from milk, then pray."
"660","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, who said: Ibn Abbas drank milk, then stood for prayer, so I said: Will you not rinse? He said: I do not care about it; be lenient, Allah will be lenient to you."
"661","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Abbas drank milk, then stood for prayer, and Mutarrif said to him: Will you not rinse? He said: I do not care about it; be lenient, you will be treated leniently. A man said: Allah says: From between dung and blood. Ibn Abbas said: And He said: Pure milk, palatable to the drinkers."
"662","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, who said: Yazid al-Rishk informed me that he heard Mutarrif ibn Abdullah saying: Ibn Abbas drank milk, then stood for prayer, so I said: Will you not rinse? He said: I do not care about it at all; be lenient, you will be treated leniently."
"663","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik and al-Harith al-A'war, that they would rinse from milk three times three times."
"664","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Muhayriz, who said: One rinses from milk."
"665","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hasan ibn Muslim narrated to me that Ibn Abbas drank fine sawiq in the Basra mosque, and al-Ghadban ibn al-Qaba'thari said to him: Will you not rinse? Ibn Abbas said: Be lenient, you will be treated leniently, and he did not rinse."
"666","From Ibn Uyaynah and Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bashir ibn Yasar, from Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khaybar; when we were at al-Sahba, and between it and Khaybar is an evening journey, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people for their provisions, and nothing was brought but sawiq, so he chewed and we chewed; then he stood and rinsed and we rinsed, and prayed Zuhr or Asr."
"667","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Do you rinse from drinks: nabidh, honey, sawiq and milk? He said: No, by Allah, I drink nabidh in the mosque and do not rinse until I pray. A man said to him: Coarse sawiq? He said: No; that is something that sticks in the mouth."
"668","From al-Thawri and Isra'il, from Abu Fazarah al-Absi, who said: Abu Zayd, freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: On the night of the jinn, two men stayed behind from them and said: We will witness Fajr with you, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have water with you? I said: I do not have water with me, but I have a waterskin with nabidh in it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A good date and purifying water, so perform wudu. Isra'il said in his hadith: Then he prayed Subh."
"669","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, who said: Do not perform wudu with milk nor nabidh."
"670","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he disliked performing wudu with milk."
"671","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who is cupped, who said: He washes the blood off him and performs wudu. I said: What if a man shaves his head and is cupped; is ghusl obligatory upon him? He said: No."
"672","From Ma'mar, from Mansur, who said: I visited Ibrahim while he was being cupped, and I said: Will you bathe today, O Abu Imran? He said: No, but I wash the trace of the cupping."
"673","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: A man bathes when he is cupped."
"674","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, both saying concerning the cupped one: He washes the trace of the cupping and performs wudu, then prays."
"675","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abu Umar, from Ibn Abbas, that he would wash the trace of the cupping."
"676","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, that Ali liked to bathe after cupping."
"677","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: I love to bathe for five: cupping, the razor, the bathhouse, janabah, and Friday. Al-A'mash said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: They did not see any ghusl as obligatory except the ghusl of janabah, and they liked ghusl on Friday."
"678","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When a man is cupped, he bathes."
"679","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said: If a man performs wudu and finishes part of his limbs while part remains, then breaks his wudu, it is a fresh wudu from the start."
"680","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: When a man breaks his wudu before completing his wudu, he restarts the wudu."
"681","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata was asked about a fringed towel: may a man wipe water with it? He refused to permit it and said: It is something introduced. I said: What if I want the towel to remove the cold of water from me? He said: Then there is no harm in it."
"682","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim and Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that both disliked the towel after wudu for prayer."
"683","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: When you perform wudu, do not dry yourself with a towel."
"684","From al-Thawri, from Qabus, from Abu Zabyan, from Ibn Abbas, that he disliked wiping with a towel after wudu, and did not dislike it when he bathed from janabah."
"685","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, that Ibn Abi Layla, Mujahid and Sa'id ibn Jubayr would dislike the towel after wudu for prayer."
"686","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab would dislike one wiping the wudu off oneself with a garment. From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Ibn al-Musayyab and Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi both disliked that."
"687","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Hakim ibn Jabir, that Hasan ibn Ali performed wudu, then called for a rag to dry with; a woman saw him and said: I saw him doing that and I hated him for it, then I saw in the night as if I were vomiting my liver in a dream."
"688","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Masruq, who said: He had a cloth with which he would dry from wudu. Al-Thawri said: And Hammad would call for a towel and dry with it."
"689","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, who said: Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal had a cloth and would dry with it when he performed wudu."
"690","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, both saying: There is no harm in wiping the wudu with a towel. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan and Maymun ibn Mihran informed me; they saw no harm in it."
"691","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ibrahim, who said: Alqamah had a clean cloth with which he would dry when he performed wudu."
"692","Al-Thaqafi reported to us, from his father, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, both saying: There is no harm in a man wiping his face from wudu before praying with a towel, or he said: with a garment."
"693","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub or another, that Ibn Sirin would wipe with a towel at wudu."
"694","From al-Thawri, who said: We heard that when a man performs wudu with water his companion already used for wudu, it does not suffice him; but if he performs wudu upon wudu, it suffices him."
"695","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: Jarir ibn Abdullah would command his family to perform wudu from the leftover of his siwak water."
"696","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: When you scratch part of your body while in wudu, then wipe it with saliva, wash that place with water. Ma'mar said: And I heard Hammad saying the like of that. Abu Bakr said: And I saw Abu Ma'mar doing that."
"697","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: He would command the tailor to wet threads with water and not wet them with his saliva."
"698","From al-Thawri, who said: It has been said concerning saliva, so take in it the easiest course."
"699","From Ma'mar, who said: I heard Qatadah, and a man asked him, saying: I put my finger in my mouth and pass it over my teeth like a siwak, then put it in my wudu water? He said: No harm."
"700","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Ja'far, from Ziyad, who said: When a vessel spends the night uncovered with no cover over it, Iblis spits in it or blows in it. I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: Or drinks from it."
"701","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man whose forearm is cut off, who said: There is no wudu upon his upper arms, but only where the wudu reaches of the upper arm."
"702","From al-Thawri, who said: If any of the wudu places remain, he washes it."
"703","And I heard Ma'mar saying: I heard that the amputated one is given wudu at his extremities."
"704","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: Whoever performs wudu, then finishes his wudu and says: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I bear witness there is no god but Allah; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You, it is sealed with a seal, then placed beneath the Throne, and not broken until the Day of Resurrection. Whoever reads Surah al-Kahf as it was revealed, then meets the Dajjal, he will have no power over him nor any way against him, and a light will be raised for him from where he reads it to Makkah."
"705","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ali, who said: When a man performs wudu, let him say: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; O Allah, make me among the repentant and make me among the purified."
"706","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, who said: Bilal wiped over his leather socks. It was said to him: What is this? He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over the khuffs and the khimar."
"707","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A man visited Bilal, or he said: Usamah, the doubt is from Abd al-Razzaq, while he was performing wudu under a spout, and he wiped over his khuffs. The man said to him: What is this? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the khuffs and the khimar. I said: What is al-math'ab? He said: The gutter-spout."
"708","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar informed me, who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu Abdillah, that he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf asking Bilal: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe over the khuffs? He said: He relieved himself, then called me with purifying water in the waterskin; he washed his face and hands, wiped over his khuffs, and said: Over his khimar, for the turban."
"709","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, who said: al-Hakam ibn Utaybah informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Bilal, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over the khuffs and over the khimar."
"710","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Bilal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wipe over the khuffs and over the khimar."
"711","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: Makhul narrated to me, from Nu'aym ibn Ziyad, who informed him, that Bilal informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wipe over the khuffs, or over the khimar, or: and the khimar; Abu Sa'id doubted."
"712","From al-Thawri, from Asim, who said: I saw Anas ibn Malik urinate, then stand and perform wudu, and wipe over his khuffs and over his turban, then stand and pray an obligatory prayer."
"713","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me, who said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform wudu while wearing the turban; he would move it back from his head without untying it, then wipe his head; he would bring water with a single palm onto the crown only, then return the turban."
"714","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah said: Two traits I ask no one about: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over the khuffs and the khimar."
"715","From Hammad, from Qatadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wipe over his turban. He said: He places his hand on his forelock, then passes his hand over the turban."
"716","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Has any concession about wiping over the turban reached you? He said: I have not heard it from anyone except Abu Sa'd al-A'ma. Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard it from Abu Sa'd al-A'ma when he narrates it."
"717","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, that he disliked wiping over the turban."
"718","From Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that he would remove the turban, then wipe his head."
"719","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Sa'id ibn Abdullah ibn Dirar, who said: I saw Anas ibn Malik go to the privy, then come out wearing a white buttoned cap, and he wiped over the cap and over his two black hair socks, then prayed. Al-Thawri said: And the cap is in place of the turban."
"720","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over his khuffs."
"721","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; when on part of the road he stepped aside and I stepped aside with him with the waterskin, and he relieved himself. Then he came to me and I poured upon his hands, and that was at Subh prayer. When he washed his face and wanted to wash his forearms, the sleeve of his cloak was tight, and upon him was a Syrian cloak. He said: So he brought his hands out from beneath the cloak and washed his forearms, then wiped over his khuffs. He said: Then we reached the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf had prayed one rak'ah with them, and I went to inform him, and he said: Leave him. Then he turned away, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed one rak'ah, and the people were startled at that, so he said: You have done right, or he said: You have done well."
"722","Ibn Jurayj reported to us, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abbad ibn Ziyad, that Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah informed him, that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah informed him, that he went on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the campaign of Tabuk. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) relieved himself before the privy-place; I carried a waterskin with him before Fajr prayer. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to me, I began pouring upon his hands from the waterskin; he washed his hands three times, then rinsed his mouth and nose, washed his face, then went to take his cloak off his forearms, but the sleeves of his cloak were tight, so he put his hands inside the cloak until he brought his forearms out from below the cloak. Then he washed his forearms to the elbows, then wiped over his khuffs. Then he came forward, and I came forward with him, until he found the people had put Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf forward; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught one of the two rak'ahs and prayed the other rak'ah with the people. When Abd al-Rahman finished, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood completing his prayer, and that startled the Muslims, and they glorified Allah much. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he turned to them, then said: You have done well, or he said: You have done right; he envied them for praying the prayer at its time. Ibn Shihab said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Hamzah ibn al-Mughirah, the like of the hadith of Abbad ibn Ziyad, and added: al-Mughirah said: I wanted to hold back Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him."
"723","From Ibn Uyaynah, who said: I heard Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd saying: Hamzah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah narrated to me, from his father, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey. He said: Stay behind, O Mughirah, and go on, O people. He said: Then he went and relieved himself, then I followed him with a waterskin of water. When he finished, I poured upon him from it; he washed his face, then went to take his hands out of a Roman cloak upon him, but the sleeves of the cloak were tight, so he brought his hands out from beneath the cloak and washed them, then wiped over his khuffs, then prayed."
"724","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; he relieved himself, then I brought a waterskin of water, and upon him was a Syrian cloak, and he could not bring his hand out of its sleeves, so he brought his hand out from below it, then wiped over his khuffs."
"725","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he urinated standing over a rubbish heap of a people, meaning their refuse, then moved aside, and I brought him water, and he performed wudu and wiped over his khuffs."
"726","Yahya ibn Rabi'ah informed us, he said: I asked Ata about wiping over the two khuffs, and he said: Three (days) for the traveler, and a day for the resident."
"727","Al-Thawri said: Wipe over them as long as your foot stays in them; and were the khuffs of the Muhajirun and the Ansar anything but torn, split and patched?"
"728","Ma'mar said: If anything comes out of it from the places of wudu, then do not wipe."
"729","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from Amir al-Sha'bi, he said: One who heard Ali informed me, and he was asked about wiping over the two khuffs, and he said: Yes, and over the two sandals, and over the khimar."
"730","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, he said: I saw Jarir urinate, then wipe over his khuffs, so he was questioned about it, and he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that. Ibrahim said: And they used to regard wiping as being after al-Ma'idah, because Jarir was the last of them to accept Islam."
"731","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, he said: I saw Jarir performing wudu from the water vessel of the mosque, and he wiped over his khuffs, and he was questioned about that, and he said: What prevents me? I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it. Ibrahim said: This hadith used to please the companions of Abdullah, because Jarir's Islam was after al-Ma'idah was revealed."
"732","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, that Jarir ibn Abd Allah said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over the two khuffs. Jarir said: And my Islam was after al-Ma'idah was revealed."
"733","From Yasin, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, from Jarir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I helped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu, and he wiped over his khuffs after al-Ma'idah was revealed."
"734","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Ibn Umar saw Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas wiping over his khuffs, so Abdullah disapproved of that. Sa'd said: Abdullah disapproved of my wiping over my khuff. Umar said: Let no misgiving occur in the mind of a Muslim man about performing wudu over his khuffs, even if he has come from relieving himself."
"735","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar said to Abdullah ibn Umar: Your uncle knows better than I do, meaning Sa'd: when you put your feet into the two khuffs while they are pure, then wipe over them, even if you come from relieving yourself."
"736","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me, from Ibn Umar, he said: I objected to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, while he was governor in Kufa, about wiping over the two khuffs, and he said: Is there any harm in that for me? And he was a resident in Kufa. Abdullah said: When he said that, I knew that he knew about that what I did not know, so I did not say anything back to him. Then we met in the presence of Umar, and Sa'd said: Ask your father about what you objected to me regarding the khuffs. So I said: What do you think, if one of us performs wudu while wearing the khuffs on his feet, is there any harm in his wiping over them? Ibn Jurayj said: And Abu al-Zubayr added to me, he said: I heard Ibn Umar narrating like the hadith of Nafi' to me, and he added, from Umar: When you put your feet into them while you are pure."
"737","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar came to Sa'd ibn Malik and saw him wiping over his khuffs, so Ibn Umar said: Do you really do this? Sa'd said: Yes. So we gathered in the presence of Umar, and Sa'd said: O Commander of the Believers, give a verdict to my nephew about wiping over the two khuffs? Umar said: When we were with our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we used to wipe over our khuffs and saw no harm in that. Ibn Umar said: Even if one comes from relieving himself and urinating? Umar said: Yes, even if one comes from relieving himself and urinating. Nafi' said: After that Ibn Umar used to wipe over them as long as he did not take them off, and he did not fix any time limit for them."
"738","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Yazid ibn Sufyan, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah, that he visited Ammar ibn Yasir after he had come out of the privy, and he performed wudu and wiped over his khuffs. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', like the hadith of Abdullah ibn Umar, up to: even if one comes from relieving himself and urinating."
"739","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, he said: When a man puts his feet into the two khuffs while they are pure, then goes to relieve himself, then performs wudu for prayer, he wipes over his khuffs. And he used to say: Umar commanded that. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"740","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: I heard a man narrating to Ibn Abbas the report of Sa'd and Ibn Umar about wiping over the two khuffs. Ibn Abbas said: If only you had said this about a long journey and severe cold."
"741","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to give fatwa for wiping over the two khuffs, but he himself would not wipe. So he was questioned about it, and he said: Do you think I give you fatwa on something whose benefit is for you and whose sin is upon me? But purification has been made beloved to me. From Ibn Jurayj, from his father, like the hadith of Ma'mar. He said: I asked Ibn Tawus: What did his father used to say about wiping over the two khuffs? He said: He used to narrate the hadith of Sa'd and Ibn Umar."
"742","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, that he saw Jabir ibn Samurah wiping over the two khuffs."
"743","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about wiping over the two khuffs, and he said: It has reached me from Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar that they used to speak of the concession of wiping over them with water, when one puts them on while they are pure. Ibn Jurayj said: So I said to Ata: Do you regard the concession of wiping over the two khuffs as being so that a man does not remove his warmth? He said: Yes."
"744","From al-Thawri, from al-Zibriqan, from Ka'b ibn Abd Allah, he said: I saw Ali urinate, then wipe over his socks and his sandals, then stand and pray."
"745","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Khalid ibn Sa'd, he said: Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari used to wipe over his socks made of hair and over his sandals."
"746","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn A'idh, from his brother, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, he said: He urinated while we were with him, then wiped over his socks and his sandals, then prayed."
"747","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abi Hayyah, from Abu al-Jallas, from Ibn Umar, that he used to wipe over his socks and his sandals."
"748","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, from Abu Mas'ud, that he used to wipe over the two socks and the two sandals."
"749","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from his father, he said: I saw al-Bara ibn Azib wiping over his socks and his sandals."
"750","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that he used to wipe over the two socks. He said: Yes, he wipes over them just like the two khuffs."
"751","Ma'mar informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, that he saw Ibrahim al-Nakha'i wiping over his two over-socks made of felt."
"752","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Mas'ud used to wipe over his khuffs and wipe over his socks."
"753","From Abu Ja'far, from Yahya al-Bakka, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: Wiping over the two socks is like wiping over the two khuffs."
"754","From Ma'mar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Zabyan al-Janbi, he said: I saw Ali urinate standing until it foamed, then perform wudu and wipe over his sandals, then enter the mosque and take off his sandals and put them in his sleeve, then pray. Ma'mar said: If I wished, I could narrate that Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did as Ali did."
"755","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zabyan, he said: I saw Ali urinate while standing until it foamed, wearing a black khamisah, then call for water and perform wudu, and wipe over his sandals, then stand and take them off, then pray Zuhr."
"756","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Qays informed me, from Abu Ishaq, that one who saw Ali wiping over his sandals informed him."
"757","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Safwan ibn Sulaym and Bakr ibn Sawadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear Sibti sandals for wudu."
"758","From Abdullah ibn Umar and Malik, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Ubayd ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: I have seen you wearing these Sibti sandals. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing them and performing wudu in them. We said to Abu Bakr: What are Sibtiyyah? He said: Sandals with no hair on them, made of cowhide. We said: Perhaps the hair goes away due to old age. He said: No, rather they are tanned that way without hair, like the Rika."
"759","Ma'mar informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Shurayh ibn Hani, he said: I asked A'ishah about wiping over the two khuffs, and she said: Ask the son of Abu Talib, for he used to travel with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we asked Ali, and he said: Three for the traveler, and a night for the resident."
"760","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Qays, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Shurayh ibn Hani, he said: I came to A'ishah asking her about wiping over the two khuffs. She said: Go to the son of Abu Talib and ask him, for he used to travel with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I went to him and asked him, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed three days and their nights for the traveler, and a night for the resident."
"761","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, from Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed three days for the traveler, and a day for the resident. By Allah, if the questioner had continued asking, he would have fixed five."
"762","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed three days for the traveler, and a day for the resident."
"763","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal asking him about wiping over the two khuffs. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us while traveling not to take off our khuffs for three days and their nights, except from janabah, but (we could keep them for) sleep, relieving oneself, and urine."
"764","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, and he said: What is your need? He said: I said: I have come seeking knowledge. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No one leaves his house seeking knowledge except that the angels spread their wings for him, pleased with what he does. I said: I have come to ask you about wiping over the two khuffs. He said: Yes, I was in the army that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent, and he commanded us to wipe over the khuffs when we had put them on in a state of purity: three when we traveled, and a night when we stayed, and not to take them off for relieving oneself, urine, or sleep, and not to take them off except from janabah."
"765","He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: In the west there is an open door whose span is seventy years; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction."
"766","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Nubatah, from Umar, he said: For the traveler three days, and for the resident a day and a night."
"767","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I came to Safwan, and he said: What has brought you? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: The angels spread their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. I said: The matter of wiping over the khuffs after relieving oneself and urinating has troubled my mind, and you were a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I have come to ask you about that: did you hear anything from him about it? He said: Yes, he used to command us, when we were on a journey or traveling, not to take off our khuffs for three days with their nights, except from janabah, but (we could keep them for) stool, urine and sleep. I said to him: Did you hear him mention love (for a people)? He said: Yes, while I was with him on a journey, a Bedouin called out to him in a loud voice, or he said: resonant, Ibn Uyaynah doubts, and said to him: O Muhammad. So he answered him with something like his speech, and said: Slow down. He said: What do you think of a man who loves a people but has not joined them? He said: He will be on the Day of Resurrection with those he loves. He said: He kept narrating to us until he said: In the direction of the west there is a door whose width is a seventy-year journey; Allah opened it for repentance on the day He created the heavens and the earth, and He will not close it until the sun rises from its direction."
"768","From Ma'mar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani, he said: We were in Adharbayjan, and Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to us to wipe over the khuffs three when we traveled, and a night when we stayed."
"769","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to the people of al-Massissah: Take off the khuffs every three (days)."
"770","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman used to say: The traveler wipes over the two khuffs for three days and their nights, and the resident for a day and a night."
"771","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ibrahim, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: Three days for the traveler, and a day for the resident."
"772","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Amr ibn al-Harith ibn al-Mustaliq, he said: I traveled with Abdullah ibn Mas'ud for three (days) to Madinah, and he did not take off his khuffs."
"773","From Isra'il, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: For the traveler three days of wiping over the two khuffs, and for the resident a day. Abu Wa'il said: And I traveled with Abdullah, and he spent three (days) wiping over the two khuffs."
"774","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Ibn Abbas, about wiping over the two khuffs, he said: Three days for the traveler, and a day for the resident."
"775","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Aban ibn Salih ibn Umayr informed me, that Ibn Shurayh informed him, that Shurayh used to say: For the resident a day until the night, and for the traveler three nights."
"776","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Wipe over the two khuffs as long as you do not take them off. He would not fix any time limit for them."
"777","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: A man wipes over his khuffs as long as he wishes, and fixes no time limit. From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, the same."
"778","From al-Thawri about wiping over the two khuffs, he said: When you put them on while pure with fresh water, then you wipe from one hadath until the same time the next day. Meaning: if you performed wudu at dawn and did not break it until Asr, then you wipe over them until Asr the next day."
"779","From Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, he said: Asim ibn Sulayman narrated to me, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I was present when Sa'd and Ibn Umar disputed before Umar about wiping over the two khuffs, and Umar said: He wipes over them until the same hour of his day and night."
"780","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: We used to wipe over them, then stand and pray. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it myself from Hisham."
"781","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Ibrahim, that he used to narrate, then wipe over his two over-socks of felt, wiping over them, then take them off, and when he stood for prayer he would wear them and pray."
"782","From Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When he takes them off, he repeats wudu; his wudu is broken."
"783","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When he takes them off, he repeats wudu; his wudu is broken. When a man wipes over his khuffs then takes them off, let him wash his feet."
"784","Al-Thawri informed me, from one of his companions, from Ibrahim, he said: When you take them off, wash your feet. And al-Thawri holds this view."
"785","From al-Thawri, from Qabus, from Abu Zabyan, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked: Which earth is purest? He said: Plowed land."
"786","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: (They should seek) clean earth. He said: The cleanest of what is around you."
"787","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How is tayammum? He said: You place the insides of your palms on the ground, then shake them off, striking one against the other, then wipe your face and your palms with a single wipe only, for the face and the palms. I said: Do I wipe over the beard along with the face? He said: Yes, along with the face."
"788","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that when he performed tayammum, he would strike with his hands once upon the dust, then wipe his face, then strike another strike, then wipe with them his hands up to the elbows, and he would not shake the dust off his hands. He said: And we hold this view. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"789","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said about tayammum: Once for the face, and once for the hands up to the elbows, and he does not shake his hands."
"790","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, and Ma'mar said it from al-Hasan also, he said: Once for the face, and once for the hands up to the elbows."
"791","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He wipes the face and the hands up to the elbows."
"792","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: It is more pleasing to me to reach up to the elbows."
"793","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: He wipes the face, and shakes his palms, striking one against the other, and wipes his palms."
"794","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman al-Khurasani, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, that Ali said about tayammum: One strike for the face, and one strike for the hands up to the wrists."
"795","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Tayammum is for the face and the palms."
"796","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He strikes the ground with his palms, then strikes with his hand, meaning he shakes it, then wipes his face and his palms."
"797","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, that Ammar ibn Yasir used to narrate that he was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and A'ishah was with him, and her necklace was lost, so the people were held up seeking it until morning came and they had no water, so tayammum was revealed. Ammar said: So they stood and wiped; they struck with their hands and wiped their faces with them, then returned and struck with their hands a second time, and wiped with them their hands up to the armpits, or he said: up to the shoulders. He said: And Ma'mar used to narrate, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, that Ammar ibn Yasir used to wipe in tayammum his face with a single wipe, then return and wipe his hands up to the armpits, and Ma'mar used to summarize it like this."
"798","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, about wiping with dust, as Allah said: he wipes his face and his hands. He said: I heard nothing from him except that."
"799","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If there is rough ground other than a sandy plain, does it suffice me? He said: Yes. He said: The sandy plain is near me; would you like to wipe from it? He said: If it is near, then soil your palms with it three times, and do not wipe the face in that, and do not shake them, then wipe your face and your palms with a single wipe only."
"800","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It is from the Sunnah that a man should not pray with tayammum except one prayer, then perform tayammum for the next prayer."
"801","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Tayammum is performed for every prayer. From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam and Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same."
"802","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Amr ibn al-As said: We renew tayammum for every prayer. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah used to hold this view."
"803","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: Tayammum is like water. Meaning: one prays with it as long as one does not break wudu."
"804","From Sa'id ibn Bashir, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib, they said: He performs tayammum and all prayers suffice him, as long as he does not break wudu; it is like water."
"805","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, he said: A single tayammum suffices, as long as he does not break wudu."
"806","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi: Tayammum may be performed with herbage and with a mountain, meaning what lies upon the mountain of dust."
"807","From al-Thawri, he said: We heard that when snow falls and one cannot reach dust with it, or there is muddy ground and one cannot reach dust, then he performs tayammum from the mane of his horse, and from his elbow, and from whatever dust there is in his cap."
"808","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: One who performs tayammum and prays, then finds water. He said: If he finds water within the time of that prayer, let him wash if he was junub, or perform wudu if he was not junub, then repeat that prayer. But if he finds water after the time of that prayer has passed, let him not repeat it, but let him wash and perform wudu for his coming prayers. From Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He repeats what was within the time."
"809","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, he said: He repeats if he finds water within the time."
"810","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: He repeats what was within the time."
"811","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: He repeats what was within the time."
"812","From Ma'mar and others, from Ibrahim, he said: When he takes them off, he repeats wudu, and his first wudu is broken."
"813","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a man performs wudu over his khuffs then takes them off, his wudu is broken."
"814","And I heard al-Thawri saying: About one who takes off one of his khuffs, he said: Washing both his feet is more beloved to us, though some of us say: he washes his foot; and the other view is more beloved to us. Al-Thawri said: When you remove the khuff from the place of wiping, wash the foot."
"815","I heard al-Thawri about a man who wore two khuffs, and over the two khuffs another two khuffs, then wiped over the upper two khuffs, then took them off while the lower two khuffs remained. He said: Wudu is broken when he takes off the upper two khuffs over which the wiping was done."
"816","From al-Thawri, about a man who wiped over his socks and wore khuffs over them, then broke wudu. He said: He takes off his khuffs and wipes over his socks."
"817","From al-Thawri, he said: It reached me from al-Hakam and Ibrahim that when they wanted to urinate while in wudu, they would put on khuffs, then stand and urinate, then perform wudu and wipe over the khuffs."
"818","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: When you take them off, repeat wudu."
"819","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I saw al-Hasan urinate, then perform wudu, and wipe over his khuffs with a single wipe over their tops. He said: So I saw the mark of his fingers on the khuff."
"820","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ala, then he said: I saw Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah urinate, then come to the Tigris and wipe over his khuffs; he wiped his fingers over the khuff, separating them. He said: So I saw the mark of his fingers on the khuff."
"821","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If you wish, wipe from the side of the leg, and if you wish, from the side of the toes toward the leg. Al-Thawri said: And I have not heard anyone speak of washing the khuff. We said to Abu Bakr: Did you see al-Thawri wiping? Or did he show you how wiping is done? He said: He showed us how wiping is done: he placed his fingers on the front of his khuff, separating them, until he reached the base of the leg from below. So Abu Bakr showed us as al-Thawri had shown him. He said: And al-Dabari showed us."
"822","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that he said: When one performs wudu over his khuffs, he places one hand above the khuff and the other beneath the khuff."
"823","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: I saw Ibn Umar wiping over them, meaning his khuffs, with a single wipe with both his hands, their insides and their outsides, after he had first poured water, and he performed wudu like this for a funeral he was called to."
"824","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Wiping over them three times is more beloved to me, as a man wipes his foot, and do not wash them. I said: Do I dip my palm in water, then not shake it until I wipe with what is in it as I wipe the head? He said: Yes. I said: What if after three wipes I miss part of the khuffs? He said: It does not harm you."
"825","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is wiping only over the uppers of the khuffs? He said: Yes. I said: Shall I not wipe the soles of the khuffs? He said: No, only their tops."
"826","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has any concession reached you about wiping with gloves, or with lifting? He said: No."
"827","I heard Sufyan about a man who performed wudu and forgot to wipe his head or some wudu spots, then put on his khuffs, then urinated. He said: He takes off his khuffs and repeats wudu, because he put them on without complete wudu. Sufyan said: About a man who performed wudu for residence and wiped over his khuffs for part of a day, for Zuhr or Asr, then decided to travel. He said: He wipes over them for the remainder of three days from what has passed. He said: And if he wiped over them while traveling for two prayers, then arrived, he completes a day and a night with what has passed of wiping; and if he wiped while traveling for a day and a night, then arrived, he takes them off when he arrives from the journey, and it becomes residence."
"828","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar informed me, from A'ishah, that she said: For my foot to be cut off is more beloved to me than to wipe over the two khuffs."
"829","Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Bishr informed us, he said: Abu Ya'qub al-Dabari narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does a feverish or sick person have a concession not to clean thoroughly and not to perform wudu fully? He said: No."
"830","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Qays informed me, from Mujahid, that he said: For a sick person with smallpox and the like, there is a concession not to perform wudu. And he recited: If you are ill or on a journey. Then he says: It is among the hidden interpretation of the Quran. And from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, the same."
"831","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: He used to say about this verse: If you are ill or on a journey, or one of you comes from relieving himself. He said: It is for a sick person struck by janabah; if he fears for himself, he has the concession of tayammum, like a traveler who does not find water."
"832","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The case of one with smallpox, does he have a concession to perform wudu? And I recited to him: If you are ill or on a journey, while he remained silent like that until: and you find no water. He said: That is when they find no water; but if they find water, let them purify themselves. He said: And if one with smallpox has a wet dream, ghusl is obligatory upon him. By Allah, I once had a wet dream, Ata the speaker, while I had smallpox, and I washed. It is for all of them when they do not find water, meaning the verse."
"833","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, he said: A man stayed as my guest and was struck by janabah while having a wound. So I asked Ubayd ibn Umayr, and he said: Let him wash what is around it, and not bring water near his wound."
"834","From Ibn Sam'an, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, that a man was struck by janabah while having wounds, and had a wet dream and sought a verdict, and they ordered him to wash, so he washed and died. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: What is wrong with you, you killed him; may Allah kill you."
"835","From al-Awza'i, from a man, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, that a man had wounds and was struck by janabah, and they ordered him, so he washed and died. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You killed him; may Allah kill you. Was not the cure for inability the question? Ata said: Then it reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wash, and leave the place of the wound."
"836","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Yahya informed me, that he heard Tawus saying: For a severely ill person there is a concession not to perform wudu, but to wipe with dust. And he said: And you find no water, then perform tayammum with clean earth. Tawus said: It is for the junub; and if you are ill, that is until: or you have touched women."
"837","Ibn Jurayj said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, that he heard him, and their statement was mentioned to him: that a sick person has a concession not to perform wudu, but that did not please him."
"838","From Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A concession for a sick person in wudu is tayammum with earth. And Ibn Abbas said: What do you think, if he were flogged? What would be done with him, as it were?"
"839","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When a person has smallpox or a wound that grows grave upon him and he fears for it, then he performs tayammum with earth. He said: And that reached me from Sa'id ibn Jubayr."
"840","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hammad, about one with smallpox and a menstruating woman, when they fear for themselves, they perform tayammum. Meaning: one with smallpox when struck by janabah."
"841","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Aban informed me, from al-Nakha'i, from Alqamah, that a man had smallpox, and Ibn Mas'ud ordered him, so dust was brought near him in a basin or vessel, and he wiped himself with dust."
"842","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from al-Nu'man ibn Rashid, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, he said: A man had smallpox and was struck by janabah, and they ordered him, so he washed and his flesh fell apart and he died. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: They killed him; may Allah kill them. Was not the cure for inability the question? He should have performed tayammum with earth."
"843","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust informed me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is a concession for a sick person to wipe with dust even while he finds water."
"844","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does a man in a cold land in Sham have a concession not to clean thoroughly and not to perform wudu fully? He said: No."
"845","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The people of Ta'if came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and complained to him of the cold, and asked him about the ghusl of janabah. He said: As for me, I pour water over my head three times."
"846","I heard al-Thawri saying: They agreed that a man in a cold land who becomes junub and fears death for himself performs tayammum, and he is like a sick person."
"847","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari informed me, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, and Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from Amr ibn al-As, that he was struck by janabah while he was commander of the army, so he left ghusl because of a verse. He said: If I wash, I will die. So he prayed with those with him while junub. When he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he informed him of what he did and told him of his excuse, and he approved and remained silent."
"848","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father or another, he said: A necklace of A'ishah fell, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a group seeking it. Morning overtook them while they had no water, so they prayed without purification. They complained of that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so tayammum was revealed. Ma'mar said: And Ayyub informed me, he said: Abu Bakr passed by A'ishah and said: You have held up the people and burdened them. Ma'mar said: And Hisham said, from his father, and Ayyub said it also, he said: When tayammum was revealed, Abu Bakr was pleased with that and said: I never knew you but blessed; no matter you disliked ever befell you except that Allah, blessed and exalted, made good in it for the Muslims."
"849","From Malik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys, until when we were at al-Bayda or Dhat al-Jaysh, my necklace broke off. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed to look for it, and the people stayed with him while they had no water. So the people came to Abu Bakr and said: Do you not see what A'ishah has done? She has held up the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people while they have no water. She said: And Abu Bakr came while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had placed his head on my thigh. He said: You have held up the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people while they are not near water and have no water with them. So Abu Bakr blamed me and said to me whatever Allah willed him to say, and he kept poking me with his hand in my side, and nothing prevented me from moving except the place of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who slept on my thigh until morning without water. So Allah revealed the verse of tayammum, and they performed tayammum. Usayd ibn Hudayr said: It is not your first blessing, O family of Abu Bakr! He said: So we sent the camel I was on, and found the necklace beneath it."
"850","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When one prays with tayammum, then finds water within the time of that prayer, he does not repeat."
"851","From al-Thawri, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, and from Ibn Shubrumah, from al-Sha'bi, they said: When one prays, then finds water within the time, he does not repeat."
"852","From Malik, from Nafi', that he came with Ibn Umar from al-Jurf; when he reached al-Mirbad and found no water, he performed tayammum with earth and prayed, and did not repeat that prayer."
"853","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad and Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar performed tayammum and prayed Asr while between him and Madinah was a mile or two, then entered Madinah while the sun was high, and did not repeat."
"854","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah informed me, that Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman said: When you are junub, wipe yourself, then when you find water, do not wash from your janabah if you wish. Abd al-Hamid said: So I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: What does he know? When you find water, wash. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me from Ali that he said that."
"855","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: He washes when he finds water."
"856","From Sa'id, from Ma'mar, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tujibi, that he asked Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman about a man who performs tayammum then finds water within the time. He said: He repeats the prayer."
"857","From al-Awza'i, he said: One of our companions informed me, he said: Two men among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were afflicted with that, then found water within the time, so they washed, or he said: performed wudu, and one of them repeated the prayer while the other did not. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him the story. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the one who repeated: You have been given your reward twice. And he said to the other: It has sufficed you."
"858","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that two men were struck by janabah, so they performed tayammum and prayed, then found water within the time, so they washed. One of them repeated the prayer, and the other did not. They asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the one who repeated: You have been given your reward twice. And he said to the other: It has sufficed you."
"859","From Muhammad ibn Yahya, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah informed me, he said: A Bedouin came to Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and said: I had a wet dream before dawn and found no water, so I performed tayammum and prayed. When morning came, I found water; shall I wash? Abu Salamah said: If you wish, wash, and if you wish, do not wash. Abu Harmalah said: So I said to Ibn al-Musayyib: Do you not hear what this one says? And I told him his statement. Ibn al-Musayyib said: Did he do that? I said: Yes. He said: So he threw pebbles toward him and said: What do you think, if one of you does not know what a fatwa is, why does he give fatwa to people? O man! You became pure for your prayer, but when you find water, ghusl is obligatory upon you."
"860","From al-Thawri, he said: When a man performs tayammum then passes by water and says: (I will wait) until I come to other water, his tayammum is broken, and he performs wudu for that prayer. And when he performs tayammum, then finds water before saying salam in his prayer, his tayammum is destroyed, and he performs wudu for that prayer."
"861","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri: May a man who does not find water perform tayammum and pray voluntary prayer? He said: No."
"862","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I relieved myself in one of these mountain passes; shall I wipe with dust and pray? He said: As for prayer, no."
"863","From al-Thawri, he said: Sufyan said: When you teach a man tayammum, that tayammum of yours does not suffice you to pray with, unless you intend by it that you perform tayammum for yourself. But when you teach him wudu, it suffices you."
"864","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man with only a small water-skin of water on a journey, struck by janabah, or the prayer time comes while he is without wudu, and he fears thirst if he purifies himself with what is in the skin. He said: Allah excuses due to excuse; he must use dust."
"865","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: When a traveler fears thirst for himself while having water, he performs tayammum. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, the same, and from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, the same."
"866","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, and from Ata, they said: When he fears thirst while having water, he performs tayammum and does not perform wudu."
"867","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man on a journey struck by janabah, having of water only enough for his wudu for prayer. He said: Let him perform wudu with it."
"868","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, about a man struck by janabah on a journey, having of water only enough for his wudu for prayer. He said: He performs wudu with it and does not perform tayammum. Ma'mar said: Performing wudu and tayammum is more pleasing to me."
"869","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, about a man struck by janabah on a journey, having no water except what he washes his face and hands with. He said: Let him wash his face and hands and pray, and not perform tayammum. Ma'mar said: And I heard another saying: Let him wash his face, and also perform tayammum."
"870","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man struck by janabah on a journey, having water, does it suffice him to wash his face and palms, while having what reaches his feet, hands and forearms? He said: No, by my life, it does not suffice him; let him not leave that when it reaches his feet, hands and forearms. Then he recited the verse of wiping, and made them both together, and made tayammum toward it if he finds no water."
"871","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is a single wipe with water more beloved to you, or three wipes with dust? He said: Rather a wipe with water; let him prefer water over dust, and if water is scant and does not suffice, let him prefer even its little over dust, covering of the ablution limbs whatever it reaches; but if water is scant he should begin by washing his private part even if it suffices only for that."
"872","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If he has with him water enough to wash his face, his feet and his forearms, may he leave the water if he wishes and wipe with dust? He said: No, by my life. I said to him: If he has only what washes his face and his private part? He said: Let him wash his face and his private part, then let him wipe his hands with dust. I said: If he has only what washes his private part? He said: Let him wash his private part, and wipe his face and his hands with dust."
"873","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata: May a man approach his wife on a journey when he has no water? He said: If water is four nights away or more, let him approach his wife; if water is three nights or less away, let him not approach his wife."
"874","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: If he will reach water the same day or tomorrow, let him not have intercourse until he reaches water; but if he is away from water with his sheep or his camels, there is no harm in his approaching his wife and performing tayammum."
"875","From Mamar, from Qatadah, concerning a man who has intercourse with his wife on a journey while he has no water, he said: There is no harm in that."
"876","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard the Bedouins asking Abu al-Shatha, saying: We stay away with our livestock for a month and two months; may one of us approach his wife when he has no water? He said: Yes."
"877","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he is on a journey, let him not approach her until he reaches water; but if he is staying away with herds, there is no harm in his approaching her even if he has no water."
"878","From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I stay in the sands for four or five months, and among us are women in postpartum bleeding, menstruating, or in major impurity; what is your view? He said: Clean earth is upon you."
"879","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu Qushayr, he said: I used to stay away from water and major impurity would befall me, so I performed tayammum, and doubt fell into my heart; so I came to Abu Dharr at his house and did not find him, then I came to the mosque, and he had been described to me, and there he was standing in prayer, so I recognized him by the description; I greeted him and he did not return my greeting until he finished, and I said: Are you Abu Dharr? He said: My family say that. I said: No one was more beloved to me to see than you. He said: You have seen me. I said: We used to stay away from water and major impurity would befall us, so we would remain days performing tayammum, and something doubtful fell into my heart about that. He said: Do you know Abu Dharr? I was in Madinah and found its climate heavy, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, assigned me some booty livestock, and I went out with it; major impurity befell me, so I performed tayammum with the earth and prayed for days, and something fell into my heart until I thought I was ruined; so I ordered a saddle-camel to be saddled, then I rode it until I came to Madinah and found the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the shade of the mosque among some of his Companions; I greeted him and he raised his head and said: Glory be to Allah, Abu Dharr? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. Major impurity befell me, so I performed tayammum for days, then something fell into my heart until I thought I was ruined; so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called for water, and a black slave-girl brought it in a large wooden cup, shaking, not full, and he said: Pure clean earth suffices as long as you do not find water, even up to ten years; when you find water, let it touch your skin. He said: And Abu Dharr's impurity had been from intercourse."
"880","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Amr ibn Bujdan, from Abu Dharr, that he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in major impurity; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called for water, screened himself and bathed, then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Pure clean earth is the ablution of the Muslim even if he does not find water for ten years; when he finds water let him make it touch his skin, for that is better."
"881","From Mamar and Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Najiyah ibn Kab, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: I became majorly impure while I was among camels, so I rolled as an animal rolls; then I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned all that, and he said: Tayammum would have sufficed you for that. Mamar said in his hadith: By Allah, I have not lied about him in the hadith."
"882","From al-Thawri, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl informed me, from Abu Malik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, he said: A man of the desert came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: O Commander of the Faithful, we remain a month and two months without finding water. Umar said: As for me, I would not pray until I find water. Ammar ibn Yasir said: Do you not remember when you and I were in such-and-such land pasturing camels, and you know that I became majorly impure? He said: Yes. So I rolled in dust, and I mentioned that to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he laughed and said: Clean earth would have sufficed you for that; that you say like this; and he struck the earth with his hand, then blew it, then wiped with them his face and his forearms up to near half the forearm. Umar said: Fear Allah, O Ammar. Ammar said: By what right you have over me, O Commander of the Faithful, if you wish I shall never mention it as long as I live. Umar said: No, by Allah; but I leave you to your affair as you were left."
"883","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: A man informed me that Abu Dharr approached his wife while he had no water, so he wiped his face and his hands, then something fell into his heart; so he went to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was three days journey from him, and found the people had prayed Fajr; he asked about the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he was out relieving himself, so he followed him; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, turned and saw him, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, inclined with his hands to the earth and placed them; he thought he said: then he shook them, then wiped with them his face and his hands, then he informed him how he had wiped."
"884","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you see His saying: or you have touched women, does it mean intercourse? He said: Yes. I said to him: The one in major impurity on a journey if he does not find water, how is his purification? He said: The purification of one who is not in ablution, if he does not find water, is the same; they do not differ; they wipe their faces and their hands."
"885","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: If a man is away from water with his camels or his sheep, there is no harm in his approaching his wife and performing tayammum. Mamar said: And I heard al-Zuhri say that."
"886","From al-Thawri and Dawud ibn Qays, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu al-Awwam, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar when a man came to him and said: I stay away with my camels; may I have intercourse when I do not find water? Ibn Umar said: As for me, I would not do that; but if you do that, fear Allah and bathe when you find water."
"887","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Umar ibn al-Khattab and a man of the Ansar to guard the Muslims; they became majorly impure when the pre-dawn cold struck them, so Umar rolled in dust, while the Ansari performed tayammum with good earth and wiped with it, then they prayed; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The Ansari was right."
"888","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard the Bedouins asking Abu al-Shatha, saying: We stay away with our livestock for a month and two months; one of us approaches his wife while he has no water. He said: Yes; he saw no harm in it."
"889","From Yahya ibn al-Araj, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, he said: If I became majorly impure and did not find water for a month, I would not pray. Sufyan said: This is not acted upon."
"890","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Sinan, from al-Dahhak, that Ibn Masud retracted his saying about the one in major impurity, that he should not pray until he bathes."
"891","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: If you become majorly impure, seek water as hard as you can; if you cannot, perform tayammum and pray, and when you can find water, bathe."
"892","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Concerning a menstruating woman who becomes pure while she has no water, he said: She performs tayammum, and her husband may approach her."
"893","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about some of that, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"894","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If a man has a wet dream in a land of snow in winter, and he believes if he bathes he will die, and he cannot prepare what to bathe with, should he bathe? He said: Yes, even if he dies. Allah said: And if you are in major impurity, purify yourselves; and Allah has made no excuse for him."
"895","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Shabi and al-Hakam about snow, and they said: Ablution is performed with it. Sufyan said: Tayammum is more beloved to me than snow, if he does not heat it."
"896","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If the one in major impurity finds nothing but snow, let him melt it; if he finds no fire and cannot perform ablution from it, then tayammum with the earth."
"897","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If major impurity befalls a man, let him wait for water; if he fears missing the prayer and no water comes, let him wipe with dust and pray."
"898","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: He waits for water as long as the time of that prayer does not pass him."
"899","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: One who was with Umar ibn al-Khattab on a journey narrated to us that major impurity befell him while he had no water, and he said: Do you think if we hasten we can reach water before sunrise? They said: Yes. So they urged their mounts, and came before sunrise, and Umar bathed and prayed. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub, the like."
"900","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: It reached me that Ali used to say: If he does not find water, let him delay tayammum until the later time."
"901","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, that his father informed him that he performed umrah with Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar camped at part of the road near some water, then had a wet dream and woke, and said: Do you think we can reach water before sunrise? They said: Yes. Mamar said: So he hastened the march; and Ibn Jurayj said: So they rode hard until they reached water, and he bathed and prayed."
"902","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar, Uthman, Aishah and the first Muhajirun used to say: If the circumcision touches the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"903","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, that Ali said: Just as the hadd is obligatory, so ghusl is obligatory."
"904","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: Al-Harith narrated to me, from Ali; and Alqamah, from Ibn Masud; and Masruq, from Aishah; they said: If the circumcision passes the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Masruq said: Aishah was the most knowledgeable of them about that."
"905","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If he sits between the four limbs, then joins circumcision to circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"906","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: If he sits between her four limbs, then exerts himself, ghusl is obligatory."
"907","From Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I asked Aishah what makes ghusl obligatory? She said: Do you know what you are like, O Abu Salamah? Like a young chick hearing the rooster crow, so it crows. If the circumcision passes the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"908","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Jafar, that Ali, Abu Bakr and Umar said: Whatever makes the two hadds obligatory, flogging or stoning, makes ghusl obligatory."
"909","From Abu Jafar, from Ali, that he used to say: It makes the hadd obligatory, yet would it not make a cup of water obligatory?"
"910","From al-Thawri, from Abu Awn, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: Does it obligate four thousand, yet not obligate a cup of water?"
"911","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Aishah said: If the two circumcisions meet, ghusl is obligatory. Ata said: My heart is not content, when the two circumcisions meet even if I do not emit fluid, until I bathe with water, because of the disagreement of the people, until I take the precautionary course."
"912","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed us, from Ibn Umar, that he used to say: If the circumcision passes the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. He said: And Aishah used to say it."
"913","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Ibn Masud: He was asked about that and said: If I penetrate, I bathe. Sufyan said: The community agrees upon ghusl."
"914","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: If the circumcision passes the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"915","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: As for me, when I am intimate with my wife, I bathe. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, the like."
"916","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, and he had met the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The saying of the Ansar, water is from water, was a concession at the beginning of Islam; then we took to ghusl after that, when circumcision touches circumcision."
"917","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, he said: I said to him: What makes ghusl obligatory? He said: Mingling and spurting. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, the like."
"918","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, differed about a man who has intercourse with his wife then withdraws from her before emitting; and he mentioned that Abu Musa al-Ashari came to Aishah and said: The disagreement of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a matter has weighed upon me; I deem you too great to face you with it. She said: What is it, repeatedly. He said: A man approaches his wife and does not emit. She said to me: If the circumcision passes the circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Abu Musa said: I shall never ask about this after you again."
"919","From Mamar, he said: One who heard Abu Jafar informed me, he says: The Muhajirun used to command ghusl, while the Ansar used to say: Water is from water; so who will decide between them? The Muhajirun said: If circumcision touches circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. So they appointed Ali ibn Abi Talib arbitrator between them, and they disputed to him; he said: What do you think, if you saw a man inserting and withdrawing, is the hadd obligatory upon him? He said: So it makes the hadd obligatory, yet would it not make a sa of water obligatory upon him? So he judged for the Muhajirun. That reached Aishah and she said: We did that at times, I and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and we rose and bathed."
"920","From Mamar, he said: I heard Hisham ibn Urwah say: I was intimate with my wife and failed to emit, so I did not bathe."
"921","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Urwah, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab narrated to me, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: Do you see, when one of us has intercourse and fails to emit? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let him wash what touched of him, and perform ablution. He said: So Abu Ayyub used to give fatwa by this, from Ubayy ibn Kab."
"922","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that he heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: If one of you has intercourse and fails, let him perform his ablution for prayer."
"923","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that Ubayy ibn Kab asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: One of us approaches the woman then fails. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Water is from water."
"924","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Mahmud, from Rashid, he said: I said to Zayd ibn Thabit: Ubayy ibn Kab used to give fatwa by that. Zayd said: Ubayy retracted that before he died."
"925","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil ibn Abi Talib, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Sad ibn Ubadah: Water is from water."
"926","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu Salih al-Zayyat informed him, from a man whom Amr names, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called a man of the Ansar, so he came out to him; they set off toward Quba and passed by a mirbad, and the Ansari bathed; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked him, and he said: You called me while I was with my wife. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If one of you fails to emit or is unsuccessful, ablution alone suffices him."
"927","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If one of you hastens or fails to emit, let him not bathe. His saying aqhata means: he does not emit."
"928","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Saib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Suad, and he was an approved man of Madinah, from Abu Ayyub, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Water is from water."
"929","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man of Banu Shayban, that he married a woman who had belonged to Rafi ibn Khadij, and she informed him that Rafi used to approach her without emitting, saying: Do not bathe; and she had sores."
"930","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ismail al-Shaybani informed me that he succeeded to a wife of Rafi ibn Khadij, and she informed him that Rafi used to practice coitus interruptus with her because of sores she had, so that she would not bathe."
"931","Ibn Uyaynah said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman then informed me, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ismail al-Shaybani, that Rafi used to say to her: You know best; if you emit, bathe."
"932","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Water is from water."
"933","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Iyad, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Khalid, he said: I asked five of the first Muhajirun, among them Ali, and each of them said: Water is from water."
"934","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Water is from water."
"935","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ismail al-Shaybani informed me that he succeeded to a wife of Rafi ibn Khadij, and she informed him that Rafi used to practice coitus interruptus with her because of sores she had, so that she would not bathe."
"936","Ibn Uyaynah said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ismail al-Shaybani, that Rafi used to say to her: You know best; if you emit, bathe."
"937","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who has intercourse with his wife elsewhere than the vagina and emits fluid, he said: He bathes, and she does not bathe, but she washes what touched of her."
"938","From Mamar, from Qatadah, and from a man, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who wakes and finds wetness? They said: Let him wash his private part and perform ablution."
"939","Mamar said: Ismail ibn Sharrus informed me, from Ikrimah, he says: Let him bathe until doubt departs."
"940","From al-Thawri, from al-Ashath, he said: I heard Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Let him bathe."
"941","From Abdullah ibn Umar, I heard him, or he informed me, from his brother Ubaydullah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If a man wakes at night and finds wetness but does not recall a dream, let him bathe; if he sees that he had a dream but finds no wetness, no ghusl is upon him."
"942","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: That is not binding, for Satan interferes."
"943","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who sees that he had a dream but finds no wetness, he said: No ghusl is upon him."
"944","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man has a dream and grasps his penis before the semen exits, holding it so it returns; is ghusl upon him? He said: If nothing exits from him, no ghusl is upon him."
"945","From Mamar, he said: Al-Ashath informed me, from al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn Mughaffal, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let none of you urinate in his bathing place, then perform ablution in it, for most whispering is from it."
"946","From al-Thawri, from one who heard Anas, he says: Urine in the bathing place brings affliction from it."
"947","From al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Whoever urinates in his bathing place has not purified."
"948","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike urinating in the bathing place? He said: No, and I urinate in it; were it a bathing place in an open plain I would dislike urinating in it; but these plastered ones, nothing settles in them, so I do not mind urinating in it; and he claimed he urinates in it."
"949","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Ata, from Aishah, she said: Allah has not purified a man who urinates in his bathing place. Layth said: Ata said: If it has an outlet, there is no harm in it."
"950","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: When I bathe from major impurity I begin with my private part, then I rinse my mouth and sniff water, then I wash my face and my hands, then I pour over my head, then with my hands, then with my foot. He said: And I wash my feet in the bathing place, then I put on sandals in it, then that suffices me and I do not wash them after. I said: What if you do not put on sandals in the bathing place and leave it barefoot? He said: Then I wash them."
"951","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, I said to Ata: The handfuls over the head, what has reached you about it? He said: It reached me that it is three. I said: What if I pour over my head three times while I have thick hair, saturating with each pouring, and I feel the need if I do not wet the roots of the hair as I wish? He said: So it is; it was said: Three times is the sunnah."
"952","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: Ghusl from major impurity is when one is thorough. He said: I said: Does he clean? He said: By it."
"953","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does the one in major impurity pass his hand over all that is visible of him? He said: Yes. A man said to him: Does the junub pour over it? He said: No, rather he bathes a bath. Does the junub wash his seat, the path of excretion, for the impurity? He said: Yes, by Allah, and that is the most deserving of what is washed of him. I said: Is not a man relieves himself and cleans, then comes and performs ablution without washing his seat? He said: Janabah occurs at times only once."
"954","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from a man called Asim, that a group came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, about what is lawful for a man of his menstruating wife, and about ghusl from major impurity; he said: As for a man's voluntary prayer in his house, it is light, so light your houses; and what good is a house with no light in it. As for what is lawful for a man of his menstruating wife: for you is what is above the waist-wrapper, and do not look upon what is beneath it until she is pure. As for ghusl from major impurity: perform your ablution for prayer, then wash your head three times, then pour water over your skin. From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Amr al-Bajali, that a party of Kufans came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: We came to ask you about three things: about a man's voluntary prayer in his house, about what is lawful for a man of his menstruating wife, and about ghusl from major impurity? He said: Are you magicians? They said: No. He said: Are you soothsayers? They said: No. He said: Where are you from? They said: From Iraq. He said: From which Iraq? They said: From the people of Kufah. He said: You have asked me about things none has asked me since I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about them. Then he mentioned the like of Mamar's hadith."
"955","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he used to say: Whoever relieves himself or is struck by major impurity, let him keep his right hand from filth and wash with his left until he cleans; then let him wash his left, then pour water over his face and his head."
"956","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, about the bathing of Abdullah ibn Umar from major impurity, he said: He would pour over his hands and wash them, then scoop with his right hand and pour over his private part, washing it with his left hand; when he finished washing his private part he washed the left, then rinsed his mouth, sniffed water and sprinkled water into his eyes, then began with his face and washed it, then his head, then his right hand, then the left, then scooped with both hands together over the rest of his body after and washed it. He said: And Abdullah ibn Umar would sprinkle water into his eyes only in ghusl from major impurity; as for ablution for prayer, no."
"957","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When he bathed from major impurity he would sprinkle water into his eyes and run fingers through his beard. He said: Abdullah said: And I know no one who sprinkled water into his eyes except Ibn Umar."
"958","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to rub his beard, and that is because I asked him about his saturating the roots of his hair."
"959","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The handfuls over the head, what has reached you about it? He said: Three has reached me about it."
"960","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: My father informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Abi Khalid, that he was asked about ghusl from major impurity, and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would pour over his head three times. He said: Then Abdullah gestured, lowering both his palms together, not gathering the fingertips to their bases, but as if spreading them somewhat, then scooping with them. He said: So he poured over his head three times. Abdullah ibn Abi Khalid traces that to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"961","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Surad al-Khuzai, from Jubayr ibn Mutim, he said: Major impurity was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: As for me, I pour over my head three times; then he gestured with his hands as if pouring with them over the head."
"962","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The junub scoops over his head three scoopfuls of water."
"963","Mamar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wanted to bathe from major impurity, he would pour over his hands, then perform his ablution for prayer, then run water through his hair until he was sure of the scalp, then pour over his head three times, then pour over the rest of his body, then take the vessel and overturn it over himself. Hisham said: But he begins with the private part, and that is not in my father's hadith."
"964","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Kurayb the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah, she said: I screened the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he bathed from major impurity; he began and washed his hands, then poured with his right over his left, washing his private part and what had touched it, then struck his hand upon the wall or the earth, then performed his ablution for prayer except his feet, then poured water over himself, then moved his feet aside and washed them."
"965","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, that Aishah informed him: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bathed from major impurity, he began and washed his hands, then performed ablution for prayer, then dipped his hand in water and ran his fingers through the roots of his hair, until when it seemed to him that he had reached the scalp of his head, he poured over his head three handfuls of water with his hands, then poured water after that over all his skin. Hisham and others do not doubt that he begins with the private part."
"966","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When Uthman bathed from major impurity he moved aside from his place and washed his feet."
"967","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Abu al-Darda was asked about the bathing of the junub, he said: Let him wet the hair and clean the skin."
"968","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Under every hair is impurity, so wet the hair and clean the skin."
"969","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: The junub pours over his hands, and performs ablution after washing his private part, then washes his head and pours over his body; when he finishes he washes his feet."
"970","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It was said: A man with thick hair scoops over his head three scoopfuls, then saturates the roots of the hair with water with each scoop."
"971","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: One with two braids, does he wet his braids? He said: No; but the roots of the hair, the scalp of the head and its skin only; but let him pour water over his head; whatever reaches his braids reaches them, and whatever misses them, no harm."
"972","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that a man came to him and asked him about ghusl from major impurity, how he washes his head? Jabir said: As for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he would scoop over his head three times. The man said: My hair is thick. Jabir said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was thicker and better than your hair."
"973","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Whoever washes his head with a washing while junub has done enough, then let him wash the rest of his body after."
"974","Abu Ishaq said, and al-Harith ibn al-Azma informed me, he said: I heard Ibn Masud say: Any junub who washes his head with khatmi has done enough."
"975","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Al-Harith ibn al-Azma met me and said: Shall I not relate to you what I heard from Abdullah? I heard him say: Any junub who washes his head with khatmi has done enough. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith ibn al-Azma, the like."
"976","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: One of them would wash his head from major impurity with lote, then wait a while, then wash the rest of his body."
"977","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: It has been established for us from Abu Hurayrah, he said: If you wash your head while junub, then wash the rest of your body after, it suffices you."
"978","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he used to say: Concerning a man who has a wife and a slave-girl, and he conceals bathing from his wife, he said: There is no harm in his washing his head, then waiting, then washing the rest of his body after, without washing his head again."
"979","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the junub washes his head with lote or khatmi while junub, let him not leave it until that dries."
"980","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, concerning a man who washes his head with khatmi while junub, then leaves it until it dries, he said: I heard Ali ibn al-Husayn say: Whatever water touches of you while you are junub, that place is purified."
"981","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Ala ibn Ziyad, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bathed one day for impurity, and saw on his shoulder a spot like a dirham's place untouched by water. He said: So he wiped it with the hair of his beard, or he said: with the hair of his head, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"982","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, concerning a man who bathes from major impurity and part of his body remains, he said: Let him wash what water did not reach."
"983","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bathed from impurity, then came out with his head dripping, while between his shoulders or above that was like a dirham's place untouched by water; someone said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Have you bathed, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: A dirham-sized place was untouched by water. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took with his palm from part of his head of what was in it and wiped it with it."
"984","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, that Ata said: If you forget a small part of the ablution limbs of the body, let water touch it."
"985","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who bathes from major impurity then sees wetness, he said: The ablution of the man and the woman is like that."
"986","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: If impurity befalls a man and he bathes, then sees wetness after urinating, let him not repeat ghusl; if he had not urinated and sees wetness, let him repeat ghusl. He said: And Said ibn Jubayr said: No ghusl except from desire."
"987","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who has a dream at night and bathes, then in the morning finds some of it on his body, he said: Let him repeat his ghusl and repeat the prayer, whether in time or out of time."
"988","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I had intercourse, then went out and found doubt before Zuhr, and did not look until I returned at night; I found dried madhi on the tip of the urethra; I ate supper and went out to the mosque, having prayed Zuhr, Asr and Maghrib, and not hastened from my supper. He said: You did right."
"989","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you see the junub bathing, and before finishing his ghusl he passes wind during his ghusl? He said: Let him wash the ablution limbs of what was washed of him, and bathe for his impurity what remains of him, and not bathe for impurity what he had already washed. He says: There is no harm in the junub passing wind during his ghusl when he performs ablution for prayer."
"990","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a junub washes his head with khatmi or lote, then goes to relieve himself, then returns; does he return to his head? He said: No, if he wishes; but let him wipe with it the wiping of ablution for prayer, then he washed."
"991","From al-Thawri, concerning a man struck by major impurity who performs ablution for prayer, then washes his head and part of his body, then passes wind before completing his ghusl, he said: Let him complete his ghusl, then repeat ablution; the occurrence nullified the ablution but did not nullify the ghusl."
"992","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said: It does not harm the junub to pass wind during his ghusl when he performs ablution for prayer."
"993","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: I and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to bathe from a single vessel, the measure of a faraq."
"994","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Aishah: That she informed him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and from her, that they both began together while junub from a single vessel."
"995","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If the man and the woman are both junub and bathe, if they wish in a vessel if they both begin dipping together; but for one to bathe with the leftover of the other, no."
"996","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if one of them dips in the vessel, then takes out his hand, and the other dips when this one takes out his hand, preceding him by only that? He said: That is dipping together; they have begun together. I said to him: If she preceded him with a scoop, then took out her hand and he dipped just then? He said: It does not harm him. I said: What if one of them scoops before the other scoops from a single vessel without finishing his ghusl in that? He said: Then they have not begun together."
"997","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that Aishah said: I and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to bathe from a single vessel while we were junub; and I used to wash the head of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in itikaf in the mosque and I was menstruating; and he used to command me when I was menstruating to wrap my waist, then embrace me."
"998","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Shatha, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah, he said: I and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to bathe from a single vessel."
"999","From Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: We used to bathe in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, men and women, from a single vessel."
"1000","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and she, used to bathe from a single vessel, each scooping from it while they were junub."
"1001","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to say: There is no harm in a man and a woman, both junub, bathing together from a single vessel."
"1002","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If one of them is junub and the other is not junub, let them not bathe together; let the one who is not junub bathe before the junub; if neither is junub, let one bathe with the leftover of the other."
"1003","Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: My knowledge and what occurs to my mind is that Abu al-Shatha informed me, that Ibn Abbas informed him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to bathe with the leftover of Maymunah; and that is because I asked him about two junubs bathing together."
"1004","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: My father would bathe then perform ablution; I would say: Does not ghusl suffice you? He said: And what ablution is more complete than ghusl for the junub; but it seems to me that something exits from my penis and I touch it, so I perform ablution for that."
"1005","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, he used to say: If you do not touch your private part after completing your ghusl, what ablution is more thorough than ghusl?"
"1006","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about ablution after ghusl, and said: What ablution is better than ghusl?"
"1007","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from a man of Ashja, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, he said: I said: Ablution apart from ghusl after impurity? He said: You have gone deep, O servant of Ashja."
"1008","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: A woman who performed ablution after ghusl was mentioned to him; he said: Had she been with me she would not have done that; and what ablution is more comprehensive than ghusl?"
"1009","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Kab, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymunah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he finished ghusl from impurity, moved aside and washed his feet."
"1010","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about ablution after ghusl, and said: No; but he washes his feet."
"1011","From Hushaym, from Jafar ibn Abi Wahshiyyah, from Abu Sufyan, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah was asked about the junub performing ablution after ghusl? He said: No, unless he wishes; ghusl suffices him."
"1012","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abdullah ibn Rafi, from Umm Salamah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman who braids her head tightly; should I undo it? He said: No; it suffices you to take with your palms three handfuls, then pour water over your skin, and you are purified."
"1013","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: The wives of Ibn Umar would not undo their heads when they bathed from major impurity and menstruation."
"1014","From Hushaym, he said: Yazid ibn Zadawayh narrated to me, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr, from Abu Hurayrah, that he asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about a woman when she bathes, does she undo her hair? Aishah said: Even if she had spent an ounce upon it? If she pours over her head three times, that suffices."
"1015","On authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah, or it reached me from him, that he used to say: A woman scoops over her head three scoopfuls. I said to Amr: And one with thick hair? He said: I see him but like her."
"1016","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Abu Bakr ibn Utbah al-Zuhri, from his uncle, from Umm Salamah, she said: one of us would leave her braid as it was when performing ghusl."
"1017","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ... , he said: A man of the Ansar told me: I knew our early women; when one of them wanted to become pure from menstruation she would comb with thin henna, then that sufficed her for her ghusl from menstruation, and she did not wash her head."
"1018","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: I sent a man called Sumayy to Ibn al-Musayyib to ask him about a woman in janabah who combs with thin henna, whether that suffices her instead of washing her head; he said: Yes; I said: Go back to him and ask him whether this is from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; Ibn al-Musayyib said: I will not go so far as to lie about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"1019","From Mamar, from a man, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman said to a daughter of his, or to his wife: Drench your head with water before Allah drenches it with Fire, whose stay upon her is short."
"1020","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: If a woman in janabah combs with thin henna, that suffices her instead of washing her head for her janabah."
"1021","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: It used to be said: A woman pours over her head three handfuls; each time she pours over her head she makes the water reach the roots of the hair, and follows with her hands until she drenches the partings of the hair."
"1022","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: A woman and a man with long hair drench their heads when they perform ghusl from janabah; and he showed me, placing both palms on his head together, then moving as if parting between the hair."
"1023","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a woman whose husband had intercourse with her and she did not perform ghusl from her janabah until she menstruated; he said: She performs ghusl from her janabah and does not wait until she becomes pure; and he had told me before that: menstruation is weightier than janabah."
"1024","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Saib, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Menstruation is greater."
"1025","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a woman whose husband had intercourse with her and she did not perform ghusl from her janabah until she menstruated; he said: She performs ghusl from her janabah; and Mamar said it from al-Hasan; from al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, likewise."
"1026","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to visit all his wives with a single ghusl."
"1027","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I saw Salman ibn Rabiah al-Bahili lean toward Umar and ask him something; we said: What did you ask him about; he said: I asked him about a man who has intercourse with his wife then wants to repeat; he said: He should perform wudu."
"1028","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from a man he named, from Jadah ibn Hubayrah, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about it, and he said: When he wants to repeat, or eat, or sleep, let him perform wudu as for prayer."
"1029","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked whether a man in janabah may warm himself with his wife while she is likewise; he said: Yes; there is no harm in a man approaching his wife twice in one janabah."
"1030","From al-Thawri, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym al-Taymi, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: I love to precede her to ghusl, so I perform ghusl, then I lie close to her until I am warm, then I order her and she performs ghusl; from al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhuluq, from Ibrahim al-Taymi: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say it and command it."
"1031","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: There is no harm in a man warming himself with his wife after he has performed ghusl from janabah before she performs ghusl."
"1032","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: he used to warm himself with her after ghusl; al-Amash said: I said to Ibrahim: Does he perform wudu after this; he said: Yes; from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: All of that was done, and refraining from it is more proper."
"1033","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that Ibn Masud used to warm himself with his wife in winter while she was in janabah though he had performed ghusl, and cool himself with her in summer while they were likewise."
"1034","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: A man in janabah has performed ghusl but his wife has not performed ghusl; may he touch her if there is a waist-wrapper over her buttocks; he said: Yes."
"1035","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, from Aishah, she said: When a man has intercourse with his wife and sleeps without performing ghusl, let him wash his private part and perform wudu as for prayer; and when he performs wudu let him do it well."
"1036","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that Aishah told him: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he wanted to sleep while in janabah would perform wudu as for prayer before sleeping; and when he wanted to eat he would wash his private part and rinse his mouth, then eat; and another added, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, in this hadith: he washed his private part, then performed wudu; that was reported to us by the Khurasani, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah."
"1037","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar: he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: May one of us sleep or eat while in janabah; he said: Yes, let him perform wudu as for prayer; Nafi said: Ibn Umar, when he wanted to do any of that, would perform wudu as for prayer except his feet; from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, similar."
"1038","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Yahya ibn Yamur, he said: I asked Aishah: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sleep while in janabah; she said: Sometimes he would perform ghusl before sleeping, and sometimes he would sleep before performing ghusl, but he would perform wudu; he said: Praise be to Allah who has made room in religion."
"1039","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi told me, from Ibn Umar: Umar asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for a ruling, saying: May one of us sleep while in janabah; he said: Yes, let him perform wudu, then sleep until he performs ghusl when he wishes; and Abd Allah ibn Umar, when he wanted to sleep while in janabah, would pour water on his hand, then wash his private part with his left hand, then wash the hand with which he washed his private part, then rinse his mouth and nose, sprinkle his eyes, wash his face and arms to the elbows, wipe his head, then sleep; and when he wanted to eat something while in janabah, he would do that."
"1040","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Ali, he said: When he wanted to eat or sleep, he would perform wudu as for prayer."
"1041","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I am in janabah and want to eat or drink, and I perform wudu, but after finishing I break wudu before eating, does the first wudu suffice me; Mamar said: Eat and drink."
"1042","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Umar: he, when he wanted to eat or sleep or drink while in janabah, would perform wudu as for prayer."
"1043","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A person in janabah washes his palms, then rinses his mouth, then eats."
"1044","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would sleep in janabah without touching water."
"1045","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd al-Yami, from Mujahid, he said: A person in janabah washes his hands and eats."
"1046","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I asked two men about janabah; one said: When I want to sleep I perform wudu and wash my private part; the other said: When I want to sleep I wash my private part, unless I want to eat."
"1047","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he wanted to eat while in janabah would wash his hands, then rinse his mouth, and eat."
"1048","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man eat before performing wudu; he said: No."
"1049","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Yahya ibn Yamur, he said: Ammar ibn Yasir came from a journey and his family scented him with yellow perfume; he said: Then I came and greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; he said: Peace be upon you, go and wash; he said: So I went and washed, then returned with its trace still on me, and said: Peace be upon you; he said: And upon you peace, go and wash; he said: So I went and took a potsherd and rubbed my skin with it until I thought I had cleaned it, then I came to him and said: Peace be upon you; he said: And upon you peace, sit; then he said: The angels do not attend the funeral of a disbeliever with good, nor a person in janabah until he performs ghusl or performs wudu as for prayer, nor one scented with yellow perfume."
"1050","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: he asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: May I sleep while in janabah; he said: Perform wudu as for prayer; and Salim said: Ibn Umar, when he wanted to sleep or eat while in janabah, would wash his private part, face and hands, adding nothing to that."
"1051","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man go out for his need while in janabah without performing wudu; he said: Yes."
"1052","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Musab ibn Sad, he said: Sad, when he became junub, would perform wudu as for prayer, then go out for his need."
"1053","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A person in janabah may be cupped, apply depilatory, trim nails, and shave his head without performing wudu; he said: Yes; and what is that, by my life, expressing surprise."
"1054","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: Aishah said: A woman asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for a ruling about a woman who has a wet dream; Aishah said to her: You have disgraced women, does a woman see that; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, turned to her and said: Then whence comes resemblance, may your right hand be dusted; and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded the woman to perform ghusl when a woman discharges; Mamar said: And I heard Hisham ibn Urwah narrate, from his father, that she was Umm Sulaym al-Ansariyyah, whose husband was Abu Talhah."
"1055","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan: Umm Sulaym, the mother of Anas ibn Malik, said: O Messenger of Allah, when does ghusl become obligatory for one of us; he said: When a woman sees what a man sees."
"1056","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that Zaynab bint Abi Salamah narrated to him, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, she said: Umm Sulaym, mother of the sons of Abu Talhah, came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, is ghusl upon a woman when she has a wet dream; he said: Yes, when she sees fluid."
"1057","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: A woman asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, a woman sees in sleep what a man sees; he said: Ghusl is upon her; Umm Salamah said: O Messenger of Allah, does a woman have a wet dream; he said: Yes, then how does her child resemble her."
"1058","From al-Thawri, he said: One who heard Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, saying: Umm Sulaym said: O Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless you, does a woman see what a man sees in sleep; Aishah said: You have disgraced women; she said: Allah is not shy of the truth; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May your hands be dusted, then whence comes resemblance."
"1059","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: When a woman has a wet dream and discharges fluid, let her perform ghusl."
"1060","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Atiq: A woman came to one of the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: A woman dreams that a man approaches her, then she wakes; when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came, his wife mentioned that to him, so he called for her and she repeated the story; he said: When she sees moisture, let her perform ghusl."
"1061","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to perform ghusl screened toward his camel; I said: Do you think it suffices me to perform ghusl toward a camel, leaving aside a mountain or rock near me; he said: Yes, your camel suffices you; I said: In the middle of my room, performing ghusl toward its middle; he said: No, but toward one of its walls; I said: With no cover on it, nothing at all, does that suffice me; he said: Yes."
"1062","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah told me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Abu Bakr, or Khalid ibn al-Walid, went to a pool outside al-Harrah and performed ghusl; they returned and told the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about their outing until they told of their ghusl; he said: How did you do it; one of them said: He screened me until I performed ghusl, then he screened him until he performed ghusl; he said: Had you done otherwise, I would have hurt you with beating."
"1063","From Ibn Jurayj, from a companion of his, from Mujahid, he said: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was at al-Hudaybiyah, wearing a garment covered over him, the wind blew and lifted the garment from him, and there was a man performing ghusl naked in the open; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, became angry and said: O people, fear Allah and be shy before the noble ones, for the angels do not leave you except at one of three: when a man is intimate with his wife, and when he is in the privy; he said: And I forgot the third; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you performs ghusl, let him conceal himself for ghusl behind a wall, or beside a camel, or let his brother screen him."
"1064","From Ismail ibn Ayyash al-Himsi, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from a man, from Ali ibn Abi Talib: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw people performing ghusl in the river naked, with no waist-wrappers on them, so he stood and called at the top of his voice: What is wrong with you, do you not hold reverence for Allah."
"1065","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: When the Kabah was being built, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abbas went carrying stones; Abbas said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Put your waist-wrapper on your neck against the stones; he did so, fell to the ground, and his eyes turned toward the sky; then he stood and said: My waist-wrapper; so his waist-wrapper was tied on him; from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from Jabir, similar."
"1066","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl: When the House was built, people were carrying stones and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was carrying with them; he took his garment and put it on his shoulder; he said: He was called out to: Do not uncover your nakedness; he said: So he threw the stone and put on his garment."
"1067","From Mamar, from Ibn Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what of our nakedness may we show and what leave; he said: Guard your nakedness except from your wife or what your right hand possesses; he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, when some of us are among others; he said: If you can that no one see your nakedness, do so; he said: I said: What if one of us is alone; he said: Allah is more deserving that one be shy before Him; and he placed his hand on his private part."
"1068","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No man shall lie skin to skin with another man, nor a woman with another woman, and it is not lawful for a man to look at the nakedness of a man, nor a woman to look at the nakedness of a woman."
"1069","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, he said: Ali came upon us while we were performing ghusl, some of us pouring on others; he said: Do you perform ghusl without screening yourselves; by Allah, I fear you are an evil after-generation, meaning by khalaf the generation in which there is evil."
"1070","From Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent Salman at the head of a detachment; he camped at the Euphrates in a tent of wool, or a cloak, and heard the voices of people, and saw they had camped at the water; he gestured with his hand thus, raising his hand and clenching his fingers, and said: By Allah, that I die then be raised, then die then be raised, is dearer to me than that I see the nakedness of a Muslim or he see mine."
"1071","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered a man and he poured a bucket of water."
"1072","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, he said: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was at al-Abwa, he came upon a man performing ghusl in the open at a basin; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, turned back and stood; when he saw him standing they came out to him from their tents; he said: Allah is modest and loves modesty, and concealing and loves concealment; so when one of you performs ghusl let him hide; then Abd Allah ibn Ubayd and Yusuf ibn al-Hakam said: He also said with that: Fear Allah; and he said: Let his brother or his boy pour over him; if there is none, let him perform ghusl toward his camel; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said a saying, all of it about that."
"1073","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out and there was a hired man of his performing ghusl in the open; he said: I do not see you shy before your Lord, take your wage, we have no need of you."
"1074","From Amir, he said: I heard that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, hired a man and saw him performing ghusl naked in the open near a ruin; he said to him: Take your wage and go away from us."
"1075","From Mamar, from Jabir al-Jufi, from al-Shabi, or from Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali: Hasan and Husayn entered the Euphrates, each with his waist-wrapper on, then they said: There is an inhabitant in the water, or: water has an inhabitant."
"1076","From Mamar, from Abu al-Zinad, from Ibn Jarhad, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw me with my thigh uncovered, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Cover it, for it is part of the nakedness."
"1077","From Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Fear Allah regarding a house called the bathhouse; they said: O Messenger of Allah, it removes dirt and benefits against such; he said: Whoever enters it, let him cover himself."
"1078","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Beware a house called the bathhouse; it was said: O Messenger of Allah, it removes dirt and benefits against such and such; he said: Whoever enters it, let him cover himself."
"1079","From al-Thawri, from Dithar, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Entering the bathhouse without a waist-wrapper is unlawful."
"1080","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, raising it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: You will conquer the non-Arabs and find houses called bathhouses; no man shall enter them except with a waist-wrapper, or he said: with a loincloth, and no woman shall enter them except postpartum or ill."
"1081","From Mamar, from Qatadah: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Musa al-Ashari: None shall enter the bathhouse except with a loincloth, and two shall not wash from one basin; from Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached him, from Umar, similar, and that one should not mention the name of Allah in it until he comes out of it."
"1082","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd Allah ibn Salamah al-Thaqafi, he said: Ali met two men who had come out of the bathhouse oiled; he said: Who are you; they said: Of the Emigrants; he said: You lie; the true emigrant is Ammar ibn Yasir."
"1083","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: al-Hasan was asked about entering the bathhouse; he said: No harm in it, if with a loincloth; they said: We see naked people in it; al-Hasan said: Islam is more honorable than that."
"1084","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he would not enter the bathhouse nor apply depilatory."
"1085","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar entered the bathhouse once wearing a waist-wrapper; when he entered he found them naked; he turned his face toward the wall, then said: Bring me my garment, O Nafi; so I brought it, he wrapped himself in it, covered his face, gave me his hand, and I led him until he came out of it, and he never entered it after that."
"1086","From Ibn Uyaynah, from an elder of Kufah, he said: Ibn Umar was asked: Why do you not enter the bathhouse, and he disliked that; he was told: You cover yourself; he said: I dislike seeing the nakedness of others."
"1087","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he applied depilatory would himself take charge of his pubic area with his hand."
"1088","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I entered the bathhouse with Abu al-Shatha and applied depilatory paste on him; I put my hands between his legs, and he said: Uff, uff, disliking that; and he himself took charge of his pubic area and groins."
"1089","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Have you ever applied depilatory in the bathhouse; he said: Yes, once."
"1090","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Women of Hims asked Aishah about entering the bathhouse, and she forbade them it."
"1091","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man of Kindah, he said: I visited Aishah with a veil between me and her; she said: Who are you from; I said: From Kindah; she said: From which army are you; I said: Of the people of Hims; she said: Of the people of Hims who make their women enter bathhouses; I said: Yes, by Allah, they do that; she said: When a Muslim woman puts off her clothes outside her husbands house, she has torn the veil between her and her Lord; if they have dared that, let each of them take a wide loose garment covering her whole body, and let no other go and describe her to a loved one or a hated one; he said: I said to her: I own nothing of her, so tell me about my need; I said: What is your need; he said: I said: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: There comes upon him an hour when he owns intercession for no one; she said: By Him, such and such, I asked him while we were in one inner garment, and he said: Yes, when the Bridge is laid, and when faces turn white and faces turn black, and at the bridge when it is heated and sharpened until it is like a sword blade, and heated until it is like an ember; the believer crosses it unharmed, but the hypocrite goes until he is in its middle, then is cut in his feet, falling on his hands toward his feet; have you seen a man walking barefoot when a thorn takes him so it nearly pierces his foot; so he falls on his hands toward his feet, then the keepers strike him with a hook on his forelock and he is thrown into Hell, falling in it for fifty years; I said: Is it heavy; he said: Heavy as five pregnant camels; then the criminals are known by their marks and seized by forelocks and feet."
"1092","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Abu Mulayh, from Aishah, she said: Women of Syria came to her; she said: Are you from the district whose women enter bathhouses; they said: Yes; she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Any woman who puts off her clothes outside her house has torn what is between her and Allah, mighty and majestic, or the veil between her and Allah, mighty and majestic."
"1093","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa told me, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah narrating it, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: he used to write to the regions: No Muslim woman shall enter the bathhouse except from illness, and teach your women Surah al-Nur."
"1094","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Qays ibn al-Harith, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Ubaydah: It reached me that women of the believing and emigrating women enter bathhouses along with women of the People of the Book; restrain that and prevent it; Abu Ubaydah, angered, though he was neither wrathful nor foul, said: O Allah, any woman who enters the bathhouse without ailment or illness, seeking thereby to whiten her face, blacken her face on the day faces turn white."
"1095","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah, he said, and I too heard it from Muhammad, from Umm Kulthum, she said: Aishah commanded me and I applied depilatory paste on her, then applied henna after it, from her parting to her foot, in the bathhouse, because of a mange she had; I said to her: Did you not forbid women; she said: I am ill, and I now forbid that any woman enter the bathhouse except from illness."
"1096","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Qays ibn al-Harith, he said: Umar wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah: It reached me that Muslim women under your charge enter the bathhouse with polytheist women; forbid that most severely, for it is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day that one outside her religion see her nakedness; Ubadah ibn Nusayy, Makhul and Sulayman used to dislike that a Muslim woman kiss a woman of the People of the Book."
"1097","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: Shall I wash with water other than bathhouse water when I come out; he said: Yes; I said to him: Hot water may be in a clean place coming out of it; he said: I do not know what woman is hidden from me; I said to him: I applied depilatory and washed in the bathhouse, does that suffice me for wudu; he said: I fear you may have missed something of wudu in between."
"1098","From Mamar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: Ali used to perform ghusl when he came out of the bathhouse; Mamar used to do it."
"1099","Israil told us, from his father, from Mujahid: Ali said: Purificatory washings are six: from janabah, from the bathhouse, from washing the dead, from cupping, ghusl for Friday, and ghusl for the two Eids."
"1100","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: I love to perform ghusl for five: cupping, the bathhouse, the razor, janabah, washing the dead, and Friday; I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: They did not regard any ghusl obligatory except janabah-ghusl, and they recommended Friday ghusl."
"1101","From Mamar and Said ibn Bashir, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Allah made water purifying, and it is not itself to be purified."
"1102","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Sharik, he said: One who heard Ibn Abbas told me: he was asked about the bathhouse, may one wash in it; he said: Yes, and leave it quickly."
"1103","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash, from Ibn Umar, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked about the bathhouse basin, may the junub and non-junub wash from it; he said: Water does not become junub."
"1104","From al-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, from al-Hazhāz, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, he said: He was asked about washing from the bathhouse; he said: Allah made water purifying, and it is not itself to be purified from."
"1105","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, he said: al-Shabi came out of the bathhouse; I said: Does one wash from the bathhouse; he said: Then why did I enter it."
"1106","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Farwah, he said: I asked al-Shabi, or he was asked: Does bathhouse-washing suffice; he said: Yes; moreover I count it the most thorough washing."
"1107","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim about reciting in the bathhouse; he said: Nothing decisive was laid down about reciting."
"1108","Abu Said Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Arabi told us, recited to him while I listened, he said: Abu Yaqub Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, he said: We read to Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talhah, from his uncle Imran ibn Talhah, from Umm Habibah: she had prolonged bleeding, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fixed her menstrual term as six or seven days."
"1109","From al-Thawri, from al-Jald ibn Ayyub, from Abu Iyas Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The term of menstruation is ten, then she is mustahadah."
"1110","From al-Thawri, from Rabi, from al-Hasan, he said: The farthest menstruation is ten."
"1111","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: A menstruating woman sees purity and purifies herself, then sees blood after it, is it menstruation; he said: No; when she sees purity let her perform ghusl; if she sees blood after it she is mustahadah; that is within her cycle days; she prays as long as she sees purity, then completes upon her cycles; if anything exceeds, it takes the status of the mustahadah, so let her pray."
"1112","From al-Thawri, about a woman whose menstruation is six days, then she menstruates two days, then becomes pure; he said: She performs ghusl and prays; if she sees menstruation after that, she refrains until she is pure up to ten; if she exceeds ten she is mustahadah and makes up the days beyond her cycle."
"1113","From Mamar, he said: She waits one day beyond her menstruation, then she is mustahadah."
"1114","From al-Thawri, about a woman whose menstruation is seven days: she remains menstruating two days, then sees purity and fasts a day, then sees blood next day, then bleeding continues to complete ten, then she is pure; she makes up that day, because she fasted it during her menstruation days; if she passes ten she is mustahadah; and he said about a woman whose cycle was six days and she exceeded her cycle up to ten: if she is pure at complete ten she does not make up prayer, but if she exceeds ten she makes up the days beyond her cycle."
"1115","Ibn Jurayj told us, from Ata, he said: A mustahadah leaves prayer for her cycle days, then waits a day, then prays; Amr ibn Dinar said that too."
"1116","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: If her cycles differ; he said: She completes upon the highest of that, then waits a day beyond the highest."
"1117","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: What is purity; he said: The light white with no yellowness or fluid with it, the light white."
"1118","Mamar told us, from Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah, he said: My mother told me that women asked Aishah about a menstruating woman performing ghusl when she sees yellowness, and praying; Aishah said: No, until she sees the white clear discharge."
"1119","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: She sees her menstruation days, with yellowness preceding the blood or fluid, is that menstruation; he said: No, and she does not leave prayer until she sees blood; I fear it is from Satan to keep her from prayer."
"1120","Mamar told us, and Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: When a woman after purity sees something suspicious like meat-washings, or fish-washings, or drops of blood before a nosebleed, it is a kick of Satans kicks in the womb; let her sprinkle with water, perform wudu and pray; Israil added in his hadith: If it is fresh flowing blood unmistakable, let her leave prayer."
"1121","From al-Thawri, from al-Qaqa, he said: I asked Ibrahim about a woman who sees yellowness; he said: She performs wudu and prays."
"1122","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: She menstruated, the blood left her, but she sees fluid or moisture; he said: She does not pray until she sees the light pure white."
"1123","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Habibah bint Jahsh, she said: I had istihadah for seven years; I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That is not menstruation, but a vein; so perform ghusl; she used to perform ghusl at every prayer, and would wash in the tub and blood would be seen in the tub."
"1124","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman with istihadah and never become pure, shall I leave prayer; the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That is only a vein, not menstruation; when menstruation comes leave prayer, and when it goes wash the blood from you, then pray; Sufyan said: Its explanation: when she sees blood after she performs ghusl, she washes the blood only; from Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah, similar; they said: She performs ghusl once a day from noon to noon at the noon prayer; from Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, similar."
"1125","From al-Thawri, from Sumayy, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I asked him about the mustahadah; he said: She sits out her cycle days, then performs ghusl from noon to noon, uses a pad, fasts, and her husband has intercourse with her."
"1126","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Qumayr wife of Masruq, from Aishah: she was asked about the mustahadah; she said: She sits out her cycle days, then performs one ghusl, and performs wudu for every prayer."
"1127","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: A mustahadah waits her cycle days, then performs one ghusl for noon and afternoon, delaying noon a little and hastening afternoon a little, likewise sunset and night, and performs ghusl for dawn; I said to him: If after noon no blood is seen until sunset but she sees moisture only; he said: She performs wudu only; she combines sunset and night."
"1128","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: She waits her cycle days, then performs one ghusl for noon and afternoon, delaying noon and hastening afternoon, and performs one ghusl for sunset and night, delaying sunset and hastening night, and performs ghusl for dawn; she does not fast, her husband does not approach her, and she does not touch the mushaf."
"1129","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr: A woman of Kufah wrote to Ibn Abbas; he gave it to his son to read and he stumbled in it, so he gave it to me and I read it; Ibn Abbas said: Had you but rattled it off as the Egyptian boy rattled; in the letter: I am a mustahadah afflicted with trial and harm; I leave prayer for a long time; Ali ibn Abi Talib was asked and ruled I perform ghusl at every prayer; Ibn Abbas said: O Allah, I find for her nothing but what Ali said, except she combines noon and afternoon with one ghusl, sunset and night with one ghusl, and performs ghusl for dawn; he was told: Kufah is a cold land and it is hard for her; he said: Had He willed He would have tried her with harder than that."
"1130","Ibn Jurayj said: from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Talhah, from his uncle Imran ibn Talhah, from his mother, daughter of Jahsh, she said: I had a heavy prolonged istihadah; I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asking his ruling; I found him at my sister Zaynabs house; I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a need of you; he said: What is it; I said: I am shy of it; he said: What is it, O woman; she said: I have a heavy prolonged istihadah preventing me prayer and fasting, what do you see; he said: I describe cotton for you, for it removes the blood; she said: It is more than that; he said: Then bind tightly with a pad; I said: It is more than that; he said: Then take a garment; I said: It is more than that, it gushes forth; he said: I command you two matters, whichever you do Allah accepts from you for the other; if you are strong for both, you know best; this is but a kick of Satans kicks; menstruate six or seven days in Allahs knowledge, then perform ghusl; when you see you are pure and certain, pray twenty-four nights and their days and fast, that suffices you; do so every month as women menstruate and purify at their times; if you can delay noon and hasten afternoon, performing one ghusl for both, then delay sunset and hasten night, performing ghusl for both and combining the two prayers, and performing ghusl with dawn, then pray; do so and fast if you can; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This is the dearer of the two matters to me; he said: Bind tightly means use a pad."
"1131","From Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Umm Salamah: she used to shed blood copiously; she asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he commanded her to perform ghusl at every prayer."
"1132","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father: A Muslim woman had istihadah; she asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or he was asked about her; he said: It is but a vein; she leaves prayer for her menstruation term, then combines noon and afternoon with one ghusl, sunset and night with one ghusl, and performs ghusl for dawn."
"1133","Mamar told us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: She waits the days she used to menstruate, then performs ghusl and prays."
"1134","From al-Thawri, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a woman wrote to him: I have had istihadah since such-and-such; I was told Ali used to say: She performs ghusl at every prayer; Ibn Abbas said: I find for her nothing but what Ali said."
"1135","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr told me that Said ibn Jubayr told him, he said: A mustahadah woman sent to Ibn al-Zubayr her boy or freedman with the message: I am afflicted, I have not prayed since such-and-such, I think he said: two years, I adjure you by Allah to clarify my religion for me; and she wrote to him: I was ruled to perform ghusl at every prayer; Ibn al-Zubayr said: I find for her nothing but that."
"1136","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked about a woman whose menstruation left her a long while, then blood returned; he said: She waits; if it is menstruation it is menstruation, and if mustahadah then it has its rule; but she should not leave prayer, when she sees blood let her perform ghusl at every prayer, then pray; then when she knows, she leaves prayer; and I fear she is mustahadah."
"1137","Ibn Jurayj told us, from Ata: He was asked about a woman whose menstruation left her thirty years, then she had istihadah; he ruled for her the rule of the mustahadah."
"1138","From Malik, from Nafi, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umm Salamah: A woman used to shed blood copiously; Umm Salamah asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for a ruling for her; he said: She waits the number of nights and days she used to menstruate before what befell her befell her; she leaves prayer for that much of the month; when she passes that, let her perform ghusl, then pad herself with a cloth, then pray."
"1139","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: If she draws forth blood, does she perform ghusl like the mustahadah; he said: No; I said: Do they differ; he said: The mustahadahs discharge comes from inside her belly."
"1140","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: A mustahadah prays and circumambulates the House."
"1141","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan, he said: She prays, and her husband approaches her; Mamar said: Qatadah said it too."
"1142","From al-Thawri, from Sumayy, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and from Yunus, from al-Hasan, they said about the mustahadah: She fasts, and her husband has intercourse with her."
"1143","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Said ibn Jubayr: he asked him about the mustahadah, may she be approached; he said: Prayer is greater than intercourse."
"1144","Mamar told us, from Ismail ibn Shuraws, he said: I heard Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, asked about the mustahadah, may her husband approach her; he said: Yes, even if blood flows on her heel."
"1145","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from al-Ajlah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: No harm in her husband having intercourse with her."
"1146","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu Jafar, he said: A woman came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: I have istihadah outside my cycle; he said: Stuff cotton, and if it recurs stuff cotton, and fast and pray, and make up what is due on you."
"1147","Mamar told us, from Ayyub, he said: Sulayman ibn Yasar was asked: May her husband approach the mustahadah; he said: We only heard of a concession for her regarding prayer."
"1148","From Mamar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said about the mustahadah: Her husband shall not approach her."
"1149","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: She does not fast, her husband does not approach her, and she does not touch the mushaf."
"1150","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: Ata was asked about the mustahadah; he said: She prays, fasts, recites Quran, pads with a cloth, then circumambulates; Sulayman ibn Musa said to him: Is it lawful for her husband to approach her; he said: Yes; Sulayman said: Opinion or knowledge; he said: We heard that when she prays and fasts, it is lawful for her husband to approach her."
"1151","From Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr: Abu Maiz Abd Allah ibn Sufyan told him he was sitting with Abd Allah ibn Umar when a woman came asking him, saying: I came wanting tawaf of the House, but at the mosque gate I gushed blood; I went back until it left me, then came again, and at the mosque gate I gushed, I did that three times; Ibn Umar said: It is a kick of Satan; perform ghusl, pad with a cloth, and circumambulate."
"1152","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: If a first-time mother is not pure in seven, then fourteen and twenty-one, the utmost being forty nights."
"1153","Mamar told us, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Abd Allah ibn Yasar, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: A first-time mother after delivery, when blood drags on, waits forty nights, then performs ghusl."
"1154","Mamar told us, from Jabir, from Khaythamah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A first-time mother after delivery, when blood drags on, waits forty nights, then performs ghusl."
"1155","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: She waits seven nights, or fourteen, then performs ghusl and prays."
"1156","Jabir said, and al-Shabi said: She waits the utmost waiting, I think he said: two months."
"1157","Ibn Jurayj told us, from Ata, and from Mamar, from Qatadah, they said: A first-time mother after delivery waits as a woman of her family."
"1158","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As: he would not approach his wives when one of them gave birth, for forty nights; Yunus said: al-Hasan said: Forty, or fifty, or forty to fifty; if more, she is mustahadah."
"1159","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: It is reported that Uthman ibn Abi al-As used to say to a wife of his when she gave birth: Do not come near me for forty nights; al-Hasan said: When forty is complete for her, she performs ghusl and prays."
"1160","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard: When she menstruates, she sits out like her female relatives; Sufyan said: Yellowness and blood in menstruation days are alike."
"1161","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there a prescribed ghusl for the menstruating woman? He said: No, except that she become thoroughly clean; she scoops over her head three scoops or more, for menstruation is more severe than janabah."
"1162","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: The woman washes her body when she becomes pure from menstruation with sidr (lote-tree leaves). I said: Should she undo her hair? He said: No; if she finds nothing but earth, it suffices her, and if she finds no water, she wipes herself with dust."
"1163","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: The menstruating woman performs ghusl just as the junub performs ghusl."
"1164","From Amir, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Muadhah, from Aishah, that she used to command women, when they became pure from menstruation, to follow the traces of the blood with yellow perfume, meaning khaluq, or with yellow dharirah."
"1165","From al-Thawri and others, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, that she said: Excellent women are the women of the Ansar; shyness did not prevent them from understanding the religion and asking about it. When Surah al-Nur was revealed, they tore the borders of their waist-wrappers and made them headcovers. And a certain woman came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah is not shy of the truth; how do I perform ghusl from menstruation? He said: Let one of you take her sidr and her water, then let her purify herself and purify herself well, then let her pour over her head and press it into the roots of her head, then let her pour over her body, then let her take a piece of musk - Abu Bakr doubted whether he said fursah or qursah - so let her purify herself with it; some said it means dharirah. She said: How do I purify myself with it? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt shy before her and veiled himself from her, and said: Glory be to Allah, purify yourself with it. Aishah said: So I grasped what he meant, took hold of the neck of her garment and said: Follow with it the traces of the blood. Abd al-Razzaq said: Lahamat means she understood."
"1166","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatadah; they said: When a pregnant woman sees blood, and her menstruation follows the measure of her cycles, she refrains from prayer just as the menstruating woman does. Mamar said: Al-Zuhri said: That is al-tarriyyah (a faint discharge)."
"1167","Mamar reported from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab, and from Amr, from al-Hasan, concerning the pregnant woman who sees blood; they said: She is in the position of the mustahadah (woman with irregular bleeding); she performs ghusl once each day at the time of the Zuhr prayer."
"1168","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When she sees (blood) after purity, she performs ghusl."
"1169","Ibn Jurayj reported; he said: I said to Ata: A woman is divorced and sees blood before she gives birth - is that menstruation? He said: No, but she is in the position of the mustahadah; she performs ghusl for every two prayers and combines them. I said: Pain overcomes her. He said: Let her perform wudu and pray until she gives birth."
"1170","From al-Thawri, from Jami ibn Abi Rashid, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, concerning the pregnant woman who sees blood; he said: She performs wudu and prays as long as she has not given birth, and even if the blood flows, there is no ghusl upon her; only wudu is upon her."
"1171","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported; he said: Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Aishah; she said: When the pregnant woman sees yellowish discharge, she performs wudu and prays, and when she sees blood, she performs ghusl and prays, and she does not abandon prayer in any case."
"1172","Mamar reported from Ayyub; he said: I wrote to Nafi that he should ask Sulayman ibn Yasar about a woman - I think he said - who was pregnant; so Nafi wrote to me that he asked him, and he said: If she sees blood, when she sees blood without a menstrual pattern and without its times, then she performs ghusl, pads herself with a cloth, and prays."
"1173","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah; she said: We did not consider yellowish and dusky discharge to be anything."
"1174","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from a man who heard Makhul saying: I asked Thawban about al-tarriyyah, and he said: There is no harm in it; she performs wudu and prays. He said: I said: Is this something you say on your own, or did you hear it? He said: His eyes filled with tears, and he said: Rather, I heard it."
"1175","From a man, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas; he said: He saw no harm in al-tarriyyah and yellowish discharge, and he saw wudu as due for it."
"1176","Ibn Jurayj reported; he said: Ata was asked about a menstruating woman for whom medicine is used so her menstruation stops, while she is in her cycle as before - may she perform tawaf? He said: Yes, when she sees purity; but if she sees agitation and does not see the white purity, then no."
"1177","Mamar reported; he said: Wasil, the freedman of Ibn Uyaynah, reported from a man who asked Ibn Umar about a woman whose menstrual bleeding was prolonged, and she wanted to drink medicine that would stop the blood from her, and Ibn Umar saw no harm in it, and Ibn Umar described the water of the arak tree. Mamar said: And I heard Ibn Abi Najih being asked about that, and he saw no harm in it."
"1178","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus; he said: I said to him: Did your father command women at the time of prayer to perform purification and remembrance? He said: No."
"1179","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: Was the menstruating woman commanded to perform wudu at the time of every prayer, then sit and remember Allah much for a while? He said: Nothing of that has reached me, though that is good. Mamar said: And it has reached me that the menstruating woman was commanded to do that at the time of every prayer."
"1180","Mamar reported from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr; she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about menstrual blood that gets on the garment; he said: Scrape it off with water, then sprinkle it and pray."
"1181","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri; he said: The menstruating woman does not have to wash her clothes, unless she wishes."
"1182","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Aishah was asked about menstrual blood washed with water whose trace does not go away; she said: Allah has made water a purifier."
"1183","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam Thabit ibn Hurmuz, from Adi ibn Dinar, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about menstrual blood that gets on the garment; he said: Wash it with water and sidr, and rub it with a rib (bone)."
"1184","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: The menstruating woman becomes pure while there is blood on her garment? He said: She washes it and sets aside her garment."
"1185","Ibn Jurayj reported; Ata reported to me, from Aishah, that she used to say: One of us would menstruate and there would be blood on her garment, so she would scrape it with a stone, or a stick, or a bone, then sprinkle it and pray."
"1186","From Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Aishah; Aishah said: One of us would wash menstrual blood with her saliva, rubbing it with her nail. He said: Whichever of these she adopted was acceptable."
"1187","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: If a menstruating woman passes by people who are reciting and they prostrate, does she prostrate with them? He said: No; she has been barred from something better than that: the prayer."
"1188","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah; they said: She prostrates."
"1189","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim; he said: When the menstruating woman and the junub hear the verse of prostration, he makes it up, for the menstruating woman does not make up the prayer."
"1190","Mamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Nudbah, a freedwoman of Maymunah; she said: I entered upon Ibn Abbas, and Maymunah had sent me to him, and there were two beds in his house, so I returned to Maymunah and said: I see Ibn Abbas as having abandoned his family. So she sent to Bint Mishrah al-Kindi, the wife of Ibn Abbas, asking her, and she said: There is no desertion between me and him, but I am menstruating. So Maymunah sent to Ibn Abbas: Do you turn away from the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would touch a menstruating wife of his while she had a cloth up to the knees, or to mid-thigh. He said: And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Shihab, from Habib, the freedman of Urwah, from Nudbah."
"1191","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salamah; she said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his blanket, then I menstruated and slipped away from him. He said: What is wrong with you, have you begun to menstruate? - meaning the menses. She said: Yes. He said: Then tie your garments upon yourself. She said: So I tied upon myself my menstrual garments, then returned and lay down with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1192","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that Umm Salamah said: I menstruated while lying with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to arrange her garments upon herself, then ordered her to lie with him on one bed while she was menstruating, with a shaqaiq cloth over her private part."
"1193","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would order me to wrap myself in an izar while I was menstruating, then he would touch me."
"1194","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim al-Bajali, that a group of the people of Kufah came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and asked him about a man praying voluntary prayers in his house, about what is permitted for a man of his menstruating wife, and about ghusl from janabah. He said: As for a man praying voluntary prayers in his house, it is light, so illuminate your houses; and what house has no light in it is of no good. As for what is permitted for a man of his menstruating wife, it is all that is above the izar; they should not uncover what is beneath it until she becomes pure. As for ghusl from janabah, perform your wudu for prayer, then pour over your head three times and rub, then pour water over your skin."
"1195","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh; he said: What is above the navel is yours. And Mamar said: And I heard Qatadah saying: What is above the izar."
"1196","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa; he said: Nafi narrated to us that Aishah said: A man may touch his wife when she is menstruating if she puts a cloth over her lower part."
"1197","From Ibn Jurayj, from Musa, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar sent to Aishah seeking her ruling about the menstruating woman, whether he may touch her. Aishah said: Yes, she should put a cloth over her lower part."
"1198","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; he said: The husband may touch the menstruating woman when her lower jazlah is covered with an izar - we heard that. Abu Bakr said: Her jazlah is from the navel to the knee."
"1199","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa; he said: What is beneath the izar of the menstruating woman is forbidden."
"1200","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father; he said: He may touch her when she is wearing her clothes."
"1201","Ibn Jurayj reported; he said: I said to Ata: May he touch her when the blood has stopped but she has not yet purified herself? He said: No, until she purifies herself."
"1202","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: The woman who has not yet purified herself is in the position of the menstruating woman until she purifies herself."
"1203","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah; he said: Aishah would comb the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in itikaf and she was menstruating. He said: He would hand her his head while she was in her room, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the mosque."
"1204","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah; she said: I would bathe with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from one vessel while we were both junub, and I would wash the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in itikaf in the mosque and I was menstruating, and he would order me while I was menstruating to wrap myself, then he would touch me."
"1205","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: Manbudh reported to me, that his mother reported to him, that while she was sitting with Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ibn Abbas entered upon her, and she said: O my son, why do I see you unkempt? He said: Umm Ammar, who combs my hair, has menstruated. She said: O my son, and what has menstruation to do with the hand? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter upon one of us while she was lying down menstruating, knowing that, and recline upon her and recite the Quran while reclining upon her; and he would enter upon her while she was sitting menstruating, and recline in her lap and recite the Quran while reclining upon her; and he would enter upon her while she was sitting menstruating, and she would spread for him the khumrah in his prayer place, and he would pray upon it in my house. O my son, and what has menstruation to do with the hand?"
"1206","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah; he said: The menstruating woman would serve my father, and he would say: Her menstruation is not in her hand."
"1207","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: Hisham ibn Urwah reported to me, from his father; he said: He was asked: Does the menstruating woman serve me, or come near me, or does a junub woman serve me? Urwah said: All of that is easy in my view, and all of that she may do for me, and there is no harm in it."
"1208","From al-Thawri, from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, from his mother, from Aishah; she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would place his head in my lap while I was menstruating, then recite the Quran."
"1209","From al-Thawri, from Miqdam ibn Shurayh ibn Hani, from his father, from Aishah; she said: I would drink from the vessel while I was menstruating, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take it and put his mouth where I had put my mouth and drink; and I would take the bone and bite from it, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take it and put his mouth where I had put my mouth and bite."
"1210","Mamar reported from Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn Masud; he said: The menstruating woman may place a thing in the mosque and take (a thing) from it."
